WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.193
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4409.

2
00:00:01.193 --> 00:00:02.386
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.386 --> 00:00:02.783
<v JENNIFER ABRAHAM CRAMER>Alright.

4
00:00:02.783 --> 00:00:03.181
Excellent.

5
00:00:03.181 --> 00:00:05.169
Thank you so much, Kyle.

6
00:00:05.169 --> 00:00:19.482
Alright, this is Jennifer Abraham Cramer and I'm here today representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with the LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries and I am . . . Today is January fourteenth the year 2015.

7
00:00:19.482 --> 00:00:25.446
I'm here today with Edward Julia Lemelle Gordwin, in her house . . . in Westlake or Mossville?

8
00:00:25.446 --> 00:00:27.036
I mean because it’s . . .

9
00:00:27.036 --> 00:00:27.434
<v EDWARD JULIA LEMELLE GORDWIN>Mossville.

10
00:00:27.434 --> 00:00:29.422
<v CRAMER>Yeah right, but it's listed . . .

11
00:00:29.422 --> 00:00:30.217
<v GORDWIN>The address . . .

12
00:00:30.217 --> 00:00:31.012
<v CRAMER>As Westlake.

13
00:00:31.012 --> 00:00:33.000
<v GORDWIN>That's the mailing route.

14
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:33.423
<v CRAMER>Okay.

15
00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:33.846
Well . . .

16
00:00:33.846 --> 00:00:36.805
<v GORDWIN>We used to have the Mossville route.

17
00:00:36.805 --> 00:00:37.228
<v CRAMER>Okay.

18
00:00:37.228 --> 00:00:38.073
<v GORDWIN>Years ago.

19
00:00:38.073 --> 00:00:47.797
<v CRAMER>Well it's appropriately we . . . Of course we’re here to discuss the Mossville community and we're here to record residents who lived in Mossville,

20
00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:53.293
and you are the very first person to be interviewed for this project.

21
00:00:53.293 --> 00:00:59.634
We really appreciate you sitting down and taking the time to talk with us today.

22
00:00:59.634 --> 00:01:01.748
So this is session one.

23
00:01:01.748 --> 00:01:13.585
I'm going to start out with just asking you some questions about growing . . . When and where you were born and growing up and all that kind of stuff.

24
00:01:13.585 --> 00:01:17.390
So tell me when and where you were born.

25
00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:25.000
<v GORDWIN>I was born on the Old Spanish Trail at Route One box 182 Mossville, 1937, January first.

26
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.471
<v CRAMER>Well then happy birthday, right?

27
00:01:27.471 --> 00:01:27.966
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

28
00:01:27.966 --> 00:01:32.414
And I . . . it was . . . I mean the first month.

29
00:01:32.414 --> 00:01:34.391
I made a mistake.

30
00:01:34.391 --> 00:01:38.345
The first month the ninth day of January.

31
00:01:38.345 --> 00:01:39.333
<v CRAMER>January ninth?

32
00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:40.322
<v GORDWIN>Ninth, ’37.

33
00:01:40.322 --> 00:01:40.816
<v CRAMER>Nineteen-thirty-seven.

34
00:01:40.816 --> 00:01:41.310
Okay.

35
00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:46.253
So you say you were born on Old Spanish Trail.

36
00:01:46.253 --> 00:01:49.218
Were you born in your house?

37
00:01:49.218 --> 00:01:52.678
<v GORDWIN>Yes, in my house with a midwife.

38
00:01:52.678 --> 00:01:55.644
We didn't go to the hospital.

39
00:01:55.644 --> 00:02:01.080
I was the fifth daughter to [Ida?] Mae and Edward Lemelle.

40
00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:04.046
Ida Mae Towner and Edward Lemelle.

41
00:02:04.046 --> 00:02:06.023
<v CRAMER>Who was the midwife?

42
00:02:06.023 --> 00:02:08.000
Do you remember?

43
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:10.975
<v GORDWIN>They call her Aunt Jane.

44
00:02:10.975 --> 00:02:13.355
That's all I know.

45
00:02:13.355 --> 00:02:14.545
Aunt Jane.

46
00:02:14.545 --> 00:02:18.116
<v CRAMER>How many siblings did you have?

47
00:02:18.116 --> 00:02:18.711
<v GORDWIN>Five.

48
00:02:18.711 --> 00:02:19.901
One brother.

49
00:02:19.901 --> 00:02:25.256
I was ten years old when he was born.

50
00:02:25.256 --> 00:02:28.826
February, he was born in February.

51
00:02:28.826 --> 00:02:37.157
<v CRAMER>And you say you were the fifth born, so who were your other siblings?

52
00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:40.727
<v GORDWIN>My four sisters. [Marian?], the oldest.

53
00:02:40.727 --> 00:02:47.868
Elaine, [Pearly?] Mae, Della Bell, and myself, and brother Edward Lemelle Junior.

54
00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:52.628
<v CRAMER>So where were Ida Mae and Edward from?

55
00:02:52.628 --> 00:02:55.603
<v GORDWIN>My mother was from Kipling,

56
00:02:55.603 --> 00:03:05.719
Louisiana that's right near Sugartown where they . . . all the melons come from now and . . . For this area.

57
00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:09.289
Daddy was born in Leonsville, Louisiana.

58
00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:12.860
That's like east of Opelousas [Louisiana].

59
00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.240
Like northeast of Opelousas.

60
00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:18.215
<v CRAMER>So what parish is that?

61
00:03:18.215 --> 00:03:20.000
<v CANDACE GORDWIN>St. Landry

62
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:20.553
<v GORDWIN>St. Landry.

63
00:03:20.553 --> 00:03:24.421
<v CRAMER>Oh, and I might want to add too that Candace Gordwin is here today.

64
00:03:24.421 --> 00:03:26.079
In case . . . so that you know . . .

65
00:03:26.079 --> 00:03:26.355
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

66
00:03:26.355 --> 00:03:29.947
<v CRAMER>I'm talking to the imaginary audience that we have here with the microphones.

67
00:03:29.947 --> 00:03:30.224
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

68
00:03:30.224 --> 00:03:32.987
<v CRAMER>That Candace is also sitting in the room with us.

69
00:03:32.987 --> 00:03:36.579
So yes, so it was Opelousas and what was it, St. Landry Parish?

70
00:03:36.579 --> 00:03:37.684
That’s what you said?

71
00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:38.789
<v GORDWIN>Yeah, St. Landry Parish.

72
00:03:38.789 --> 00:03:41.000
<v CRAMER>So what brought them down this way?

73
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.643
<v GORDWIN>I think the time that growth and opportunity.

74
00:03:44.643 --> 00:03:49.196
Mother was . . . came here when she was . . . In the ‘20s.

75
00:03:49.196 --> 00:03:51.472
She was a little girl.

76
00:03:51.472 --> 00:03:53.749
They came by wagon train.

77
00:03:53.749 --> 00:03:58.757
She said their thing was pulled by six spans of mules.

78
00:03:58.757 --> 00:04:06.953
They camped overnight from Kipling to here and they went up to the West Fork River in Westlake.

79
00:04:06.953 --> 00:04:09.230
That's where they stayed first.

80
00:04:09.230 --> 00:04:19.247
Then they moved to Mossville, what we always called Houston River Road when we were growing up, is what they called it.

81
00:04:19.247 --> 00:04:30.174
Then after the migration and the animals, I think that start traveling and the times that it was all like prairie land, Mother said.

82
00:04:30.174 --> 00:04:38.370
She later . . . The shrubbery start growing and the little trees were coming up and they called it Saprack.

83
00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:42.923
So now when we say, "Oh we was in Saprack."

84
00:04:42.923 --> 00:04:46.111
No one knows what we're talking about.

85
00:04:46.111 --> 00:04:47.932
It was the saplings.

86
00:04:47.932 --> 00:04:50.664
They talk about the little trees.

87
00:04:50.664 --> 00:04:53.396
That’s what they called it, Saprack.

88
00:04:53.396 --> 00:04:56.128
When I was a little girl,

89
00:04:56.128 --> 00:05:06.145
I can remember looking out across the prairie and the road have a . . . the Old Spanish Trail have a curve in it,

90
00:05:06.145 --> 00:05:09.787
about a half a mile from my house.

91
00:05:09.787 --> 00:05:15.251
I could stand in the backyard and look at the hogs grazing.

92
00:05:15.251 --> 00:05:17.983
The animals grazing in the yard.

93
00:05:17.983 --> 00:05:25.268
They used to call that area Shoat’s Prairie because they had a lot of hogs there.

94
00:05:25.268 --> 00:05:26.179
Shoat’s Prairie.

95
00:05:26.179 --> 00:05:31.643
I remember that we used to . . . Everybody got together and helped everybody.

96
00:05:31.643 --> 00:05:38.017
We would raise a barn over to someone's house, over to say the Hartman's,

97
00:05:38.017 --> 00:05:43.936
and all the men would get together and go help raise the barn.

98
00:05:43.936 --> 00:05:48.945
The ladies would get together and cook and bring the food.

99
00:05:48.945 --> 00:05:51.221
I remember we'd dig wells.

100
00:05:51.221 --> 00:05:53.043
They would dig wells.

101
00:05:53.043 --> 00:05:58.962
The same thing . . . They dug wells by hand and they made boxes around,

102
00:05:58.962 --> 00:06:08.068
like wooden boxes down in as they dug it to hold the dirt back and they built wells like that.

103
00:06:08.068 --> 00:06:09.889
That's the same thing.

104
00:06:09.889 --> 00:06:12.166
We'd have the big gathering.

105
00:06:12.166 --> 00:06:13.077
Everybody came.

106
00:06:13.077 --> 00:06:19.451
They fed the men, and the women always cooked and brought baskets of stuff.

107
00:06:19.451 --> 00:06:24.915
<c.p>And I can remember Mother always would help everybody in the community.

108
00:06:24.915 --> 00:06:27.647
She was strong [forward?] going lady.

109
00:06:27.647 --> 00:06:30.379
Mother always was there for everybody.

110
00:06:30.379 --> 00:06:33.111
Everybody came to her for everything.

111
00:06:33.111 --> 00:06:35.843
She helped people that were sick.

112
00:06:35.843 --> 00:06:44.949
You would see her going . . . She would have her little bucket with her cleaning rags in it and her Lysol,

113
00:06:44.949 --> 00:06:49.502
the old Lysol, and it would . . . She would clean up.

114
00:06:49.502 --> 00:06:56.787
She would take it with her everywhere . . . The lye, the old time lye, and wash clothes.

115
00:06:56.787 --> 00:06:59.064
She would make a fire.

116
00:06:59.064 --> 00:07:04.983
Boil the clothes in the pot and they would . . . with a big paddle.

117
00:07:04.983 --> 00:07:09.081
They would stir them around so they'd be clean.

118
00:07:09.081 --> 00:07:11.813
I guess they were sterilizing them.

119
00:07:11.813 --> 00:07:15.000
So she would always do that.

120
00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:21.323
Then in the afternoon, she would have a . . . they would . . .We would go to a quilting bee sometimes.

121
00:07:21.323 --> 00:07:30.455
They had all the quilting things hung in the ceiling and all the ladies would be around the quilting and they would . . . Everybody sew and quilted,

122
00:07:30.455 --> 00:07:31.860
and I'm just watching.

123
00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.319
Mother used to go to Ms. Hartman's.

124
00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:43.100
Laura Hartman, she was born a Prater, and she used to go to her house and take care of her when she was getting older.

125
00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:46.262
Mother would fix her breakfast and cook for her.

126
00:07:46.262 --> 00:07:53.638
I would always eat the oatmeal and Mother would just leave it in the little pot that she cooked it in.

127
00:07:53.638 --> 00:07:58.907
I would be sitting there eating the oatmeal and . . . We would always call her Granny.

128
00:07:58.907 --> 00:08:02.771
And Granny said, "You better quit eating out of that pot.

129
00:08:02.771 --> 00:08:04.527
You're going to get fat."

130
00:08:04.527 --> 00:08:06.283
Sure enough I got fat.

131
00:08:06.283 --> 00:08:09.093
I always was the fattest child Mother had.

132
00:08:09.093 --> 00:08:12.606
But I think that's because I liked to eat everything.

133
00:08:12.606 --> 00:08:13.659
I loved food.

134
00:08:13.659 --> 00:08:16.118
I loved all kind of food [laughs].

135
00:08:16.118 --> 00:08:18.577
So we always had a good time.

136
00:08:18.577 --> 00:08:23.495
As I grew up my sisters were in school, I was home with Mother.

137
00:08:23.495 --> 00:08:30.520
I tell them all the time I had more working when I was with my mom than any of them,

138
00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:35.437
because after they all grew up and got married I was home with Mother.

139
00:08:35.437 --> 00:08:36.842
There . . . Butch was there.

140
00:08:36.842 --> 00:08:40.706
My little brother Edward Lemelle Junior, we always called him Butch.

141
00:08:40.706 --> 00:08:43.516
So he was home then to take attention.

142
00:08:43.516 --> 00:08:44.219
And Mother

143
00:08:44.219 --> 00:08:56.864
<c.p>and I . . . Though when we were by ourselves before Butch was born we would go to the pen where the cow . . . Early in the morning and Mother would have buckets on the wheelbarrow and with water,

144
00:08:56.864 --> 00:09:00.376
and she'd wash the cow’s bags off with warm water.

145
00:09:00.376 --> 00:09:06.699
And she’d say . . . I say, "Mama, why you washing?' She said, "That makes the milk come down baby.

146
00:09:06.699 --> 00:09:11.265
You warm it and it'll make the milk come down and it's clean."

147
00:09:11.265 --> 00:09:18.993
She would wash the cow’s bag off and then she would rinse it, the soap off, and she would milk the cow.

148
00:09:18.993 --> 00:09:24.964
And always wrapped . . . She always wrapped a biscuit and it would be hot in a little cloth.

149
00:09:24.964 --> 00:09:28.828
And when she'd get some . . . the first milk she would get,

150
00:09:28.828 --> 00:09:36.907
she would have my little cup and I would drink the milk and eat my biscuits with the homemade preserves she had put.

151
00:09:36.907 --> 00:09:39.014
I always liked to eat [laughs].

152
00:09:39.014 --> 00:09:49.903
So then we would do that and go back to the house and she would pour the milk up . . . They used to say strain the milk for any trash or anything.

153
00:09:49.903 --> 00:09:55.523
She would pour it in a big crock bowl out of the pot and sterilize it.

154
00:09:55.523 --> 00:09:57.631
And see, it would sit there.

155
00:09:57.631 --> 00:10:03.251
Some sit to make . . . I know now it was like yogurt, but we called it clabber.

156
00:10:03.251 --> 00:10:06.763
The milk would clabber, get hard, and we'd eat that.

157
00:10:06.763 --> 00:10:08.520
Some . . . And everybody ate theirs.

158
00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:10.627
I wanted sugar on mine [laughs].

159
00:10:10.627 --> 00:10:13.789
We would eat . . . I'd eat it with the sugar.

160
00:10:13.789 --> 00:10:19.057
The older girls, "We're not eating any sugar because we don't want to get fat."

161
00:10:19.057 --> 00:10:21.516
They would eat it plain like that.

162
00:10:21.516 --> 00:10:24.677
Then about nineteen . . . That was in the early ’40s.

163
00:10:24.677 --> 00:10:27.839
In forty . . . About '42 I know the war came.

164
00:10:27.839 --> 00:10:31.000
They was having war and Daddy . . . [phone rings].

165
00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.381
<v CRAMER>We can pause it for a second [break in tape].

166
00:10:33.381 --> 00:10:37.667
So before we get to the war . . . We interrupted there for just a second because of the phone.

167
00:10:37.667 --> 00:10:41.714
Before we get to the war I kind of want to go back a little bit further . . .

168
00:10:41.714 --> 00:10:41.952
<v GORDWIN>Okay.

169
00:10:41.952 --> 00:10:42.905
<v CRAMER>If you don't mind.

170
00:10:42.905 --> 00:10:45.524
So I got a couple questions for you regarding your grandparents.

171
00:10:45.524 --> 00:10:51.000
Can you talk to us a little bit about your grandparents’ names and where they were from and that sort of thing?

172
00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:51.424
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

173
00:10:51.424 --> 00:10:57.784
Grandfather . . . I found here they've been doing . . . One of my first cousins [Arty Irving?] Taylor.

174
00:10:57.784 --> 00:11:03.720
She's been doing some research on the internet like where my grandfather was from.

175
00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:09.656
They found out that he was from Cameroon, Africa and his mother was Chinese.

176
00:11:09.656 --> 00:11:11.352
And Grandfather was short.

177
00:11:11.352 --> 00:11:18.136
We always wondered where we got the eyes . . . like most of us . . .I have a sister,

178
00:11:18.136 --> 00:11:29.584
she's real . . . her daughter one of the kids they reached back and took that and they always said . . . When she was in school up at Louisiana Tech,

179
00:11:29.584 --> 00:11:31.704
they thought she was Chinese.

180
00:11:31.704 --> 00:11:36.792
One of the girls up there . . . They were real prejudice up there.

181
00:11:36.792 --> 00:11:38.488
I talk this freely.

182
00:11:38.488 --> 00:11:45.272
It doesn't bother me, because I know what I am and I know who I am.

183
00:11:45.272 --> 00:11:47.816
They would say . . . They told her,

184
00:11:47.816 --> 00:11:54.600
"We don't like black people and you come sit by me you're Chinese you're not . . . "Oh,

185
00:11:54.600 --> 00:12:00.112
and my niece looked at them and said, "Oh no, I'm not Chinese.

186
00:12:00.112 --> 00:12:07.744
I'm nigger-nese." [laughs] Because they said that bad word to her that she . . . They didn't like black people,

187
00:12:07.744 --> 00:12:18.344
but they said the n-word and she say, "Oh well if you don't like them you don't like me because I'm that kind of nese."

188
00:12:18.344 --> 00:12:20.040
<v CRAMER>Now who was that?

189
00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:21.312
<v GORDWIN>That was Cathy.

190
00:12:21.312 --> 00:12:23.008
<v CRAMER>And that is your . . .?

191
00:12:23.008 --> 00:12:23.856
<v GORDWIN>My niece.

192
00:12:23.856 --> 00:12:25.552
<v CRAMER>Oh so your niece.

193
00:12:25.552 --> 00:12:30.216
Now so who were your . . . who . . . What was your grandmother's name?

194
00:12:30.216 --> 00:12:32.760
Like your grandmother on your mother’s . . .

195
00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.032
<v GORDWIN>[Irie?] Viola Towner.

196
00:12:34.032 --> 00:12:34.880
<v CRAMER>Alright and . . .

197
00:12:34.880 --> 00:12:37.000
<v GORDWIN>Irie Viola Potts Towner.

198
00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:38.966
<v CRAMER>Okay, and your grandfather?

199
00:12:38.966 --> 00:12:40.932
<v GORDWIN>Was David Duff Towner.

200
00:12:40.932 --> 00:12:46.831
<v CRAMER>Okay and they were the one . . . They were from St. Landry Parish?

201
00:12:46.831 --> 00:12:49.780
<v GORDWIN>No, no. They were from Sugartown.

202
00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:50.271
<v CRAMER>Sugartown.

203
00:12:50.271 --> 00:12:50.763
Okay.

204
00:12:50.763 --> 00:12:52.729
<v GORDWIN>That's my mother's people.

205
00:12:52.729 --> 00:12:56.169
<v GORDWIN>My daddy's family was from St. Landry.

206
00:12:56.169 --> 00:12:58.136
<v C. GORDWIN>Sugartown is Beauregard Parish.

207
00:12:58.136 --> 00:12:59.119
<v CRAMER>Oh, okay.

208
00:12:59.119 --> 00:13:00.102
Got you.

209
00:13:00.102 --> 00:13:01.576
<v C. GORDWIN>That Beauregard Parish.

210
00:13:01.576 --> 00:13:02.559
<v CRAMER>Beauregard Parish.

211
00:13:02.559 --> 00:13:04.034
Alright and then . . .

212
00:13:04.034 --> 00:13:06.000
<v C. GORDWIN>Kipling, Beauregard Parish.

213
00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:07.312
<v CRAMER>So your grandparents . . .

214
00:13:07.312 --> 00:13:09.500
<v C. GORDWIN>That's on the maternal side.

215
00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.125
<v CRAMER>Yes, so on your maternal side.

216
00:13:12.125 --> 00:13:14.750
Now what about your paternal side?

217
00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:16.938
What about your dad's parents?

218
00:13:16.938 --> 00:13:18.688
What were their names?

219
00:13:18.688 --> 00:13:21.750
<v GORDWIN>His mother was named Marie Louis Lemelle.

220
00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:22.188
<v CRAMER>Okay.

221
00:13:22.188 --> 00:13:23.062
<v C. GORDWIN>Jean Louis.

222
00:13:23.062 --> 00:13:27.000
<v GORDWIN>Well it's French they would say, "Jean Louis."

223
00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:27.372
<v CRAMER>Okay.

224
00:13:27.372 --> 00:13:30.721
<v GORDWIN>Jean Louis or [saying with French accent] Jean Louis.

225
00:13:30.721 --> 00:13:31.465
<v C. GORDWIN>Jean Louis.

226
00:13:31.465 --> 00:13:31.837
J-E-A-N.

227
00:13:31.837 --> 00:13:33.698
<v CRAMER>I thought Lemelle seemed French.

228
00:13:33.698 --> 00:13:34.814
<v GORDWIN>It is French.

229
00:13:34.814 --> 00:13:37.419
<v CRAMER>With the lowercase L-E and then M-E-L-L-E.

230
00:13:37.419 --> 00:13:37.791
<v GORDWIN>L-L-E.

231
00:13:37.791 --> 00:13:38.163
Yes.

232
00:13:38.163 --> 00:13:39.651
<v CRAMER>So that's your dad's . . .

233
00:13:39.651 --> 00:13:40.023
<v C. GORDWIN>Mother.

234
00:13:40.023 --> 00:13:42.628
<v CRAMER>And then your mom's were the Towners.

235
00:13:42.628 --> 00:13:43.744
Okay, got it.

236
00:13:43.744 --> 00:13:44.488
Got it.

237
00:13:44.488 --> 00:13:45.233
Got it.

238
00:13:45.233 --> 00:13:45.977
Got it.

239
00:13:45.977 --> 00:13:53.791
<c.p>And you said they . . . that your mom and dad . . . Your mom's family and your dad's family both moved here for opportunity.

240
00:13:53.791 --> 00:13:59.000
So what kind . . . Do you know what kind of opportunity that that was?

241
00:13:59.000 --> 00:13:59.891
<v GORDWIN>Well, working.

242
00:13:59.891 --> 00:14:06.127
Making a living to even just build a home to have your own space.

243
00:14:06.127 --> 00:14:17.708
Daddy and them had acres and acres of land down there, but he wanted to come this way because it was nothing to do but farm.

244
00:14:17.708 --> 00:14:26.171
They were trying . . . and his uncle lived in Lake Charles and he had a night club and a . . . Well,

245
00:14:26.171 --> 00:14:28.399
they used to say saloon.

246
00:14:28.399 --> 00:14:32.408
He had a saloon and a cleaners for clothes.

247
00:14:32.408 --> 00:14:37.307
Daddy went, moved there and was working there on the boulevard.

248
00:14:37.307 --> 00:14:39.980
Uncle Gilbert was well established there.

249
00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:43.098
His people now still talk about him.

250
00:14:43.098 --> 00:14:47.107
That he was the thing then, Uncle Gilbert Lemelle.

251
00:14:47.107 --> 00:14:49.780
And he . . . Daddy worked for him.

252
00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:56.461
Daddy used to deliver the clothes and run errands for him and work for him.

253
00:14:56.461 --> 00:15:02.252
Then Daddy was working for the sawmill of the [Bell?] and Garrison . . . well,

254
00:15:02.252 --> 00:15:08.042
which is now . . . It turned to be Garrison, but it was Bell then.

255
00:15:08.042 --> 00:15:14.724
And the [Gauses?] and they were all married to . . . Bells married in with the [Gauses?].

256
00:15:14.724 --> 00:15:18.287
And he married . . . he was . . . He married Mother.

257
00:15:18.287 --> 00:15:25.860
Mother was . . . had moved from there and she . . . from out here into Lake Charles with her godmother,

258
00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:29.869
which was . . . We would always call her Nanny [Coin?].

259
00:15:29.869 --> 00:15:34.323
She was a real like fashionable lady and outstanding lady.

260
00:15:34.323 --> 00:15:35.659
Very Christian woman.

261
00:15:35.659 --> 00:15:41.004
Mother stayed with her and was working in Lake Charles doing housework.

262
00:15:41.004 --> 00:15:52.140
So Daddy was at the sawmill and that's where he . . . Mr. Bell saw him and he said Daddy was always such a fine looking gentleman,

263
00:15:52.140 --> 00:15:59.267
and a fine gentleman, that he shouldn't be doing that kind of work he was doing.

264
00:15:59.267 --> 00:16:04.167
He brought him to his home to be his head butler.

265
00:16:04.167 --> 00:16:09.512
Daddy was there and Daddy and Mama Bell got along real well.

266
00:16:09.512 --> 00:16:22.430
And they were on the corner of Mill and Moss and Daddy stayed there on premises with his family and they were all born there but me and my

267
00:16:22.430 --> 00:16:22.875
<c.p>brother.

268
00:16:22.875 --> 00:16:27.775
But my brother was born at the hospital at Saint Patrick.

269
00:16:27.775 --> 00:16:36.238
I'm the only one that was born on the Old Spanish Trail in the house that we lived in.

270
00:16:36.238 --> 00:16:42.029
So Daddy . . . Mother says she met Daddy at Sacred Heart at a dance.

271
00:16:42.029 --> 00:16:46.483
They would have the dances at the Sacred Heart Hall.

272
00:16:46.483 --> 00:16:51.829
She said when she saw him walk in, she said, "That's mine.

273
00:16:51.829 --> 00:16:54.501
I'm going to marry that man.

274
00:16:54.501 --> 00:16:57.619
That's going to be my children's father."

275
00:16:57.619 --> 00:17:00.737
Say he was so tall and handsome.

276
00:17:00.737 --> 00:17:03.410
He said they danced and danced.

277
00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:06.082
And they dated for a while.

278
00:17:06.082 --> 00:17:10.537
Then he just . . . He had cousins live in Orange, Texas.

279
00:17:10.537 --> 00:17:15.437
He stole her and went away to Orange, Texas and married.

280
00:17:15.437 --> 00:17:19.000
They eloped and here their license here.

281
00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:20.275
<v CRAMER>Oh my goodness!

282
00:17:20.275 --> 00:17:21.124
Nineteen twenty-five.

283
00:17:21.124 --> 00:17:22.399
October of 1925.

284
00:17:22.399 --> 00:17:23.673
That is amazing.

285
00:17:23.673 --> 00:17:28.346
Kyle you're going to have to get a photograph of this.

286
00:17:28.346 --> 00:17:33.869
Oh my gosh, and there's a picture of the church in the background.

287
00:17:33.869 --> 00:17:34.719
<v GORDWIN>No that . . .

288
00:17:34.719 --> 00:17:36.843
<v CRAMER>Or an illustration of it.

289
00:17:36.843 --> 00:17:38.542
<v GORDWIN>That's just the thing.

290
00:17:38.542 --> 00:17:41.092
That's how the license were made.

291
00:17:41.092 --> 00:17:47.039
I don't know why they . . . That's how they put them out then I guess.

292
00:17:47.039 --> 00:17:50.438
Daddy probably bought a pretty one for Mother.

293
00:17:50.438 --> 00:17:52.137
Probably had a choice.

294
00:17:52.137 --> 00:17:53.412
<v CRAMER>That is awesome.

295
00:17:53.412 --> 00:17:55.961
Do you have pictures of them?

296
00:17:55.961 --> 00:17:56.810
<v GORDWIN>Oh, yes!

297
00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:57.660
<v CRAMER>Okay great.

298
00:17:57.660 --> 00:18:02.758
We want to kind of look at those a little bit later.

299
00:18:02.758 --> 00:18:07.431
So they met, had a whirlwind courtship, and then they eloped.

300
00:18:07.431 --> 00:18:07.856
<v GORDWIN>Eloped.

301
00:18:07.856 --> 00:18:08.706
<v CRAMER>In 1925.

302
00:18:08.706 --> 00:18:09.131
<v GORDWIN>Five.

303
00:18:09.131 --> 00:18:15.078
Mother was twenty years old, and Daddy was a few years older than Mother.

304
00:18:15.078 --> 00:18:19.752
<v C. GORDWIN>He was born in 1898, and she was born in 1903.

305
00:18:19.752 --> 00:18:20.176
<v CRAMER>Okay.

306
00:18:20.176 --> 00:18:21.026
Thank you.

307
00:18:21.026 --> 00:18:24.000
That was Candace by the way.

308
00:18:24.000 --> 00:18:24.362
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

309
00:18:24.362 --> 00:18:29.799
She always . . . They always sit here and fill in where I forget to say things.

310
00:18:29.799 --> 00:18:30.524
<v CRAMER>That's perfect.

311
00:18:30.524 --> 00:18:35.961
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees] And I've taught them all that . . . Tell them the history so they would know.

312
00:18:35.961 --> 00:18:41.760
And so we . . . when I was growing up we would even put our clothes . . . The well,

313
00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:43.934
it was like a shallow well.

314
00:18:43.934 --> 00:18:49.734
We didn't want to use all the good drinking water washing and doing laundry and stuff.

315
00:18:49.734 --> 00:18:54.808
So Mother would . . . We would load up the wheelbarrows or either the slide according

316
00:18:54.808 --> 00:19:04.594
<c.p>to how many clothes we had and we would hook the slide up with the horse and we'd go down where it used to be the brimstone.

317
00:19:04.594 --> 00:19:06.769
Right . . . We called it the brimstone.

318
00:19:06.769 --> 00:19:11.843
It's where they used to have railroad that would bring the . . . From Sulphur [Louisiana].

319
00:19:11.843 --> 00:19:14.017
The sulfur from the sulfur mines.

320
00:19:14.017 --> 00:19:19.454
We would load up and go down the . . . Round the railroad had nice clear water.

321
00:19:19.454 --> 00:19:23.803
The ditches were fresh and was no sewage or anything in them.

322
00:19:23.803 --> 00:19:25.253
Just fresh rain water.

323
00:19:25.253 --> 00:19:28.153
We would go there and wash the clothes.

324
00:19:28.153 --> 00:19:32.140
We even had lines made out there to hang the clothes.

325
00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:39.751
As they dried we'd wash them and hang them and fold them and so . . . And Mother always had three rinsing tubs.

326
00:19:39.751 --> 00:19:43.376
She would rinse them in three different tubs of water.

327
00:19:43.376 --> 00:19:47.000
<v CRAMER>To make sure all that soap was out right?

328
00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:47.341
<v GORDWIN>Right.

329
00:19:47.341 --> 00:19:48.706
She had that bluing.

330
00:19:48.706 --> 00:19:52.460
It's a little bottle of blue stuff, she'd call it, “bluing.”

331
00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:54.849
She'd put it in her last water.

332
00:19:54.849 --> 00:19:59.627
You couldn't rinse the yellow clothes in that because it make them look dingy.

333
00:19:59.627 --> 00:20:02.698
The white clothes, and the blue clothes, and colors.

334
00:20:02.698 --> 00:20:04.063
You washed everything separate.

335
00:20:04.063 --> 00:20:07.817
You didn't wash every color together like the kids do now.

336
00:20:07.817 --> 00:20:11.571
You separate them to wash the whites by themselves and everything.

337
00:20:11.571 --> 00:20:17.714
The dish towels washed by themselves, and she would soak them in that lye water and sterilize them.

338
00:20:17.714 --> 00:20:22.833
She would always boil them in the pot and be sure that they were clean.

339
00:20:22.833 --> 00:20:26.246
She didn't wash her dishtowels with the underclothes and things.

340
00:20:26.246 --> 00:20:34.437
She was always so clean until I used to tell the kids, "When I grow up I'm never going to clean up my house."

341
00:20:34.437 --> 00:20:39.897
Because Mother make us clean up so much now and you had to clean the baseboards.

342
00:20:39.897 --> 00:20:43.992
You had to get on your knees and clean the base boards.

343
00:20:43.992 --> 00:20:49.794
Scrub the old wooden floors with a brush and a little wire that scrubbed up the floors.

344
00:20:49.794 --> 00:20:51.500
They had to be white.

345
00:20:51.500 --> 00:20:54.913
It had a man came in Mother's house one day.

346
00:20:54.913 --> 00:20:58.667
He was . . . came to see . . . That was the first I remember

347
00:20:58.667 --> 00:20:59.690
<c.p>getting rugs in.

348
00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:04.127
He said . . . He came to measure and he said, "My God Ms. Lemelle.

349
00:21:04.127 --> 00:21:09.587
Your floors are so clean you could just put the food and eat off the floors."

350
00:21:09.587 --> 00:21:16.071
We would have to scrub them with the soap and then she'd make us rinse them with clear water.

351
00:21:16.071 --> 00:21:21.532
Then we would wipe it . . . Had to wipe it up and be sure it got clean.

352
00:21:21.532 --> 00:21:26.992
You better not put . . . leave no . . . Don't leave no mud in them cracks because the dust.

353
00:21:26.992 --> 00:21:31.429
She’d say, “Sweep that floor good before you put the water on it.”

354
00:21:31.429 --> 00:21:32.794
She was so clean.

355
00:21:32.794 --> 00:21:39.278
We had to wash dishes and be sure to clean everything good before you go to bed that night.

356
00:21:39.278 --> 00:21:42.008
We did like all our work at night.

357
00:21:42.008 --> 00:21:48.151
All we had to do the next morning: get up, put the clothes on, and go to school,

358
00:21:48.151 --> 00:21:56.000
and brush the teeth, and get your hair combed, take a bath and everything before you go to school in the morning.

359
00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:02.744
We had a big old stove in the kitchen and it had . . . We call it a reservoir on it.

360
00:22:02.744 --> 00:22:12.326
It was a wooden stove and you would . . . We would have to fill it in the afternoon with water and it stayed hot while Mother was cooking.

361
00:22:12.326 --> 00:22:13.746
It would stay hot.

362
00:22:13.746 --> 00:22:17.650
We would get the water from there to take a bath.

363
00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:21.554
We would put more water in as we take it out.

364
00:22:21.554 --> 00:22:29.008
And by the time it was time for Daddy to get his bath the water was warm, hot again for him.

365
00:22:29.008 --> 00:22:35.396
We would go to the garden I remember, me and Mother, and pick mustard greens and gather vegetables.

366
00:22:35.396 --> 00:22:41.785
She would put her . . . The sweet potatoes that Daddy had planted and we'd dug and put up harvest.

367
00:22:41.785 --> 00:22:51.013
She would go get the sweet potatoes . . . We had a bin under the house that we put like Irish potatoes and sweet potatoes in to make.

368
00:22:51.013 --> 00:22:53.142
It was like a tin thing.

369
00:22:53.142 --> 00:22:57.047
The house was like four or five feet off the ground.

370
00:22:57.047 --> 00:22:59.531
I could get under there real well.

371
00:22:59.531 --> 00:23:05.565
She would . . . He'd build that with tin all around it and he would put hay in it.

372
00:23:05.565 --> 00:23:10.534
And we would go underneath there and get the vegetables out, the sweet potatoes,

373
00:23:10.534 --> 00:23:15.148
and we would wash them and put them in the oven to bake.

374
00:23:15.148 --> 00:23:22.956
The wooden stove, she'd start it off and while we were gone to the garden to gather the greens they were baking.

375
00:23:22.956 --> 00:23:24.731
We'd come back in and

376
00:23:24.731 --> 00:23:29.345
<c.p>of course when I got back I wanted to eat a sweet potato.

377
00:23:29.345 --> 00:23:36.088
We would . . . Mother would work in her yard all day long planting flowers and doing everything in the yard.

378
00:23:36.088 --> 00:23:36.798
Cutting grass.

379
00:23:36.798 --> 00:23:40.702
We had a little push lawnmower we would cut grass with.

380
00:23:40.702 --> 00:23:42.832
One year it snowed in March.

381
00:23:42.832 --> 00:23:50.640
We had sweet peas on the fence, so pretty, and Mother went and start gathering them when it was getting so cold.

382
00:23:50.640 --> 00:23:54.189
She got . . . She had sweet peas all over our house.

383
00:23:54.189 --> 00:23:56.674
Just flowers just all over the house.

384
00:23:56.674 --> 00:24:04.482
So she said, "Oh my goodness it looks like somebody died in here, but I'm not going to let my flowers freeze."

385
00:24:04.482 --> 00:24:11.935
I can remember the girls were getting ready to go on a picnic for school for their vacation time for Easter.

386
00:24:11.935 --> 00:24:13.000
<v CRAMER>Your sisters?

387
00:24:13.000 --> 00:24:15.958
<v GORDWIN>Yes, and I was home of course.

388
00:24:15.958 --> 00:24:19.339
I wasn't old enough to go to school.

389
00:24:19.339 --> 00:24:24.411
But they couldn't go on they picnic because it snowed that March.

390
00:24:24.411 --> 00:24:31.595
So they . . . Everybody was so glad to see snow because it didn't snow all the time here.

391
00:24:31.595 --> 00:24:36.244
And so . . . And it was really a plenty snow that year.

392
00:24:36.244 --> 00:24:41.315
Then I know in the ’40s Daddy . . . The war was going on.

393
00:24:41.315 --> 00:24:49.345
Mother . . . When the boys were called from here to go, it was two Moss men went to the war,

394
00:24:49.345 --> 00:24:56.952
two Edwards went to war, my uncle Fredrick Towner went to war, and a Hartman went to war.

395
00:24:56.952 --> 00:25:01.179
Mother would give each . . . And a Vincent went to war.

396
00:25:01.179 --> 00:25:04.982
Mother gave them a big party in the yard.

397
00:25:04.982 --> 00:25:07.518
We didn't have electricity out here.

398
00:25:07.518 --> 00:25:15.970
They put posts down and lit the bottle lamps and all around had the yard all lit up that night.

399
00:25:15.970 --> 00:25:21.464
They had a big, big dinner for them and served food and everything.

400
00:25:21.464 --> 00:25:27.804
I remember she made homemade ice cream and she had wooden barrels of punch made.

401
00:25:27.804 --> 00:25:33.298
I remember they said, "Kids can't have no punch out of that one."

402
00:25:33.298 --> 00:25:37.101
They had rum in that big barrel of punch.

403
00:25:37.101 --> 00:25:39.637
So we . . . That went on fine.

404
00:25:39.637 --> 00:25:43.440
I drank so much and the men . . . I'm greedy.

405
00:25:43.440 --> 00:25:45.976
You know I want to eat.

406
00:25:45.976 --> 00:25:46.399
I'm

407
00:25:46.399 --> 00:25:58.655
<c.p>picking the fruit out of the punch with the rum was in and eating it and I got drunk. [Laughs] I was so sick I was just throwing up.

408
00:25:58.655 --> 00:26:03.726
I was stealing it and Mother didn't see what I was doing.

409
00:26:03.726 --> 00:26:08.375
So she said, "Oh I know what you been doing now.

410
00:26:08.375 --> 00:26:12.179
You been eating that fruit out of that punch."

411
00:26:12.179 --> 00:26:19.363
And so that was a big laugh for everybody because I got drunk off of the fruit.

412
00:26:19.363 --> 00:26:28.238
And so, [to her daughter] Kim mama slap Charles if he don't quit making that funny face to make me laugh.

413
00:26:28.238 --> 00:26:35.000
And so that's where we had the party and everybody went off to the war.

414
00:26:35.000 --> 00:26:38.688
Then we were . . . I would listen on the radio every day.

415
00:26:38.688 --> 00:26:43.717
Daddy was working in his garden and he would . . . I would listen to the news.

416
00:26:43.717 --> 00:26:50.757
I would run to the garden and tell Daddy what was being said and old reporter then was Mr. Walter [Winchell?].

417
00:26:50.757 --> 00:26:56.121
He would go like the Morse code, "Beep, beep, beep, beep, beep, beep, beep, beep, beep.

418
00:26:56.121 --> 00:27:03.162
Mr. and Mrs. North America your ships is out to sea and they pushed the Germans back so many latitudes back.

419
00:27:03.162 --> 00:27:07.855
So many . . ." I said, "Oh I didn't know what it meant,” but Daddy did.

420
00:27:07.855 --> 00:27:14.561
I'm just . . . And he would tell everything and I would tell him word for word what the news reporter said.

421
00:27:14.561 --> 00:27:15.902
He said, "Okay baby."

422
00:27:15.902 --> 00:27:18.584
I didn't know how far away that was.

423
00:27:18.584 --> 00:27:20.260
I thought it was close.

424
00:27:20.260 --> 00:27:22.942
We had to have blackouts . . . We had blackouts.

425
00:27:22.942 --> 00:27:25.624
We had to put all the lights out.

426
00:27:25.624 --> 00:27:32.329
Then here come a man through the community saying that a refinery . . . He was looking at everything and he said,

427
00:27:32.329 --> 00:27:37.694
"Y'all could be rich here if you just had the money to put up oil fields."

428
00:27:37.694 --> 00:27:44.399
He say, "Anywhere you see marshy land with lot of pine cones and pine trees, you have lots of oil."

429
00:27:44.399 --> 00:27:46.746
I remember it was in the ’40s.

430
00:27:46.746 --> 00:27:51.104
Not long after that, Cities Service . . . My daddy went to work down there.

431
00:27:51.104 --> 00:27:54.457
Daddy's in a book that they did at Cities Service.

432
00:27:54.457 --> 00:27:58.480
They don't know who it is, but I know who it was.

433
00:27:58.480 --> 00:27:59.821
That was my daddy.

434
00:27:59.821 --> 00:28:08.538
He was standing up with the shovel and I could tell by his hat and the way he did it; he always rolled his pants up.

435
00:28:08.538 --> 00:28:11.890
He never let his pants like drag in the mud.

436
00:28:11.890 --> 00:28:13.566
He would roll them up.

437
00:28:13.566 --> 00:28:14.908
He was standing there.

438
00:28:14.908 --> 00:28:18.595
Then a picture of my husband when they developing in Maplewood

439
00:28:18.595 --> 00:28:22.618
<c.p>[housing for Cities Service Refinery in Sulphur, Louisiana] was doing that slick.

440
00:28:22.618 --> 00:28:25.636
They didn't have tractors and stuff like that, backhoes.

441
00:28:25.636 --> 00:28:28.653
He was doing the dirt like with the horse.

442
00:28:28.653 --> 00:28:30.329
He was managing a horse.

443
00:28:30.329 --> 00:28:34.017
He could do it because he was raised on a farm.

444
00:28:34.017 --> 00:28:39.382
They were doing it like they would . . . It was a thing made something like a wheelbarrow,

445
00:28:39.382 --> 00:28:42.399
but it was closed all in to the back.

446
00:28:42.399 --> 00:28:48.769
And you pick it . . . On the front it would scoop up the dirt when the horse would pull it,

447
00:28:48.769 --> 00:28:53.127
and then they would take it and dump it where they wanted to.

448
00:28:53.127 --> 00:29:03.520
So he was doing that because he knew . . . His mother told me when I married him that he had been plowing and things ever since he was a little bitty boy.

449
00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:08.214
She say, "You just could see his head over that bar on the plow."

450
00:29:08.214 --> 00:29:10.561
So he was raised on the farm.

451
00:29:10.561 --> 00:29:17.601
And that's why he left home and went to war early because he wanted to do something else besides just that.

452
00:29:17.601 --> 00:29:22.630
He was making a better life for his self when he went to the navy.

453
00:29:22.630 --> 00:29:24.977
But he was only seventeen years old.

454
00:29:24.977 --> 00:29:27.324
He was too young to be there.

455
00:29:27.324 --> 00:29:29.000
<v CRAMER>Now who was this?

456
00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:30.542
<v GORDWIN>That was my husband.

457
00:29:30.542 --> 00:29:30.927
<v CRAMER>Okay.

458
00:29:30.927 --> 00:29:34.010
Now, so he went to war at seventeen.

459
00:29:34.010 --> 00:29:37.479
Where did he . . . What theater did he serve in?

460
00:29:37.479 --> 00:29:40.177
Did he go to Europe or Asia?

461
00:29:40.177 --> 00:29:40.562
<v GORDWIN>Europe.

462
00:29:40.562 --> 00:29:44.802
He was in foreign war and he was from Lemoine, Louisiana.

463
00:29:44.802 --> 00:29:46.729
That's in St. Landry Parish.

464
00:29:46.729 --> 00:29:48.271
He was only seventeen.

465
00:29:48.271 --> 00:29:57.135
Now when he come back, he had that post traumatic syndrome and it lasted and lasted all through me being married to him.

466
00:29:57.135 --> 00:29:57.521
Eventually . . .

467
00:29:57.521 --> 00:30:00.990
<v KIM MCKEE>Which branch, she say, Mama did he work in?

468
00:30:00.990 --> 00:30:01.375
<v GORDWIN>Pardon?

469
00:30:01.375 --> 00:30:02.146
<v MCKEE>What branch?

470
00:30:02.146 --> 00:30:02.917
<v C. GORDWIN>The navy.

471
00:30:02.917 --> 00:30:06.000
<v GORDWIN>He was in the navy coast guard.

472
00:30:06.000 --> 00:30:06.707
<v CRAMER>So what . . .

473
00:30:06.707 --> 00:30:08.474
<v GORDWIN>And they carried the ammunition.

474
00:30:08.474 --> 00:30:08.828
<v C. GORDWIN>Cargo.

475
00:30:08.828 --> 00:30:10.242
<v GORDWIN>Cargo to the soldiers.

476
00:30:10.242 --> 00:30:14.130
He said he was in a bad battle on the Rhine.

477
00:30:14.130 --> 00:30:19.079
He said he just saw blood running from the hills on the Rhine River.

478
00:30:19.079 --> 00:30:25.442
That's where he always . . . And when he was working at Citgo . . . I'm kind of ahead of myself though,

479
00:30:25.442 --> 00:30:30.037
but when he was working at Citgo . . . Well, it was Cities Service then.

480
00:30:30.037 --> 00:30:39.228
They had a bad explosion and he had to go out there and clean up pieces of people and he said all came back to him.

481
00:30:39.228 --> 00:30:40.995
He got sick after that.

482
00:30:40.995 --> 00:30:43.116
He was . . . Had that post traumatic.

483
00:30:43.116 --> 00:30:50.540
He was nervous, nervous . . . he couldn't hardly . . . The doctor said he was too young to see such things that he saw.

484
00:30:50.540 --> 00:30:53.721
And so he said it just stayed with him.

485
00:30:53.721 --> 00:30:55.135
Said it had a

486
00:30:55.135 --> 00:30:56.902
<c.p>lot of soldiers like that.

487
00:30:56.902 --> 00:30:57.609
So, but . . .

488
00:30:57.609 --> 00:30:59.023
<v MCKEE>Getting back to Mossville.

489
00:30:59.023 --> 00:31:00.437
<v GORDWIN>Getting back to Mossville.

490
00:31:00.437 --> 00:31:01.144
That happened.

491
00:31:01.144 --> 00:31:02.205
We were here.

492
00:31:02.205 --> 00:31:03.619
I was living here.

493
00:31:03.619 --> 00:31:05.386
He built me this house.

494
00:31:05.386 --> 00:31:11.042
<v CRAMER>So you guys were already married by the time he . . . Or did you know each other?

495
00:31:11.042 --> 00:31:12.809
<v GORDWIN>No, married after the war.

496
00:31:12.809 --> 00:31:14.223
Way after the war.

497
00:31:14.223 --> 00:31:16.698
<v CRAMER>So did you know each other before?

498
00:31:16.698 --> 00:31:22.000
<v GORDWIN>No, no, no, no. I didn't know him until I was finishing high school.

499
00:31:22.000 --> 00:31:22.409
<v CRAMER>So . . .

500
00:31:22.409 --> 00:31:26.494
<v GORDWIN>But back to Daddy, and Mother, and Grandfather, and Grandmother.

501
00:31:26.494 --> 00:31:37.117
Grandmother and them lived up on what they call Evergreen Road now, but we would call it Houston River Road back in that time in Saprack.

502
00:31:37.117 --> 00:31:40.794
Grandmother and them had a big house back there.

503
00:31:40.794 --> 00:31:44.471
<v MCKEE>That's where they coming with the plant at, Jennifer.

504
00:31:44.471 --> 00:31:45.696
Around that area.

505
00:31:45.696 --> 00:31:51.825
<v GORDWIN>That's where the refinery going to be because my nephew . . . One of my first cousins,

506
00:31:51.825 --> 00:31:55.502
was living there and they bought them out first.

507
00:31:55.502 --> 00:32:01.222
And so all the white people . . . All this here was owned by former slaves.

508
00:32:01.222 --> 00:32:03.673
This land here was former slaves.

509
00:32:03.673 --> 00:32:11.027
After the Emancipation Proclamation they was free to come and that’s where they . . . And this was outlaw country.

510
00:32:11.027 --> 00:32:14.296
I've been studying history said nobody wanted this.

511
00:32:14.296 --> 00:32:18.381
Nobody wanted this area because it was like outlaw country.

512
00:32:18.381 --> 00:32:24.918
So swampy, so wet, so nothing could . . . They didn't think they could do anything with it.

513
00:32:24.918 --> 00:32:37.992
So this where they could get and they have . . . I'm going to try to get him to bring . . . A Vincent man I know he move here in the ’80s from Washington, D.C.

514
00:32:37.992 --> 00:32:47.389
His daddy was born and raised here up on . . . I can take you to the place and show you where he was born.

515
00:32:47.389 --> 00:32:55.152
I might remember it since they didn't develop everything so, but he had acres and acres of land, too.

516
00:32:55.152 --> 00:33:01.689
He was one of the . . . I think he was probably about the second person in here.

517
00:33:01.689 --> 00:33:04.957
The Mosses was first and they were second.

518
00:33:04.957 --> 00:33:05.366
And . . .

519
00:33:05.366 --> 00:33:07.000
<v MCKEE>Go ahead Jennifer.

520
00:33:07.000 --> 00:33:11.683
<v CRAMER>I was just thinking . . . I was wondering if you could kind of describe the community boundaries.

