00:33:11.683 --> 00:33:13.439
Like so where does Mossville begin?

522
00:33:13.439 --> 00:33:14.902
How far does it go?

523
00:33:14.902 --> 00:33:18.415
<v GORDWIN>As far as I know it began where the school is there.

524
00:33:18.415 --> 00:33:19.000
<v CRAMER>Okay.

525
00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:27.171
<v GORDWIN>And back to what we used to call Perkin Town almost to Sulphur.

526
00:33:27.171 --> 00:33:27.800
<v CRAMER>Okay.

527
00:33:27.800 --> 00:33:37.857
<v GORDWIN>I can take you there and show you if you would want to see the boundary.

528
00:33:37.857 --> 00:33:41.000
<v MCKEE>Okay, but this Jennifer. . .

529
00:33:41.000 --> 00:33:41.626
<v GORDWIN>And back to . . .

530
00:33:41.626 --> 00:33:42.670
<v MCKEE>Before the school was built?

531
00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:46.635
How far back do you want to know where Mossville was then when she came up as a child?

532
00:33:46.635 --> 00:33:50.183
<v CRAMER>Yeah I mean I'm just trying to get a picture for people who might not be familiar . . .

533
00:33:50.183 --> 00:33:50.391
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

534
00:33:50.391 --> 00:33:50.809
<v CRAMER>. . . with Mossville.

535
00:33:50.809 --> 00:33:51.852
This is kind of a . . .

536
00:33:51.852 --> 00:33:53.522
<v GORDWIN>It was there always and back to Saprack.

537
00:33:53.522 --> 00:33:56.443
Back there it was Vincent land, and Perkin, and Prater, and all that land.

538
00:33:56.443 --> 00:33:57.278
Back where they doing . . .

539
00:33:57.278 --> 00:33:59.991
<v MCKEE>Because you used to tell us, Mama, something about the Bel Air area.

540
00:33:59.991 --> 00:34:01.243
That's why I'm asking was Mossville . . .

541
00:34:01.243 --> 00:34:02.287
<v GORDWIN>That was the Mitchell's [agrees].

542
00:34:02.287 --> 00:34:03.330
That was the Mitchell’s land.

543
00:34:03.330 --> 00:34:05.000
<v MCKEE>Yeah was that considered Mossville back then?

544
00:34:05.000 --> 00:34:05.288
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

545
00:34:05.288 --> 00:34:05.862
<v MCKEE>Okay from . . .

546
00:34:05.862 --> 00:34:06.725
<v GORDWIN>First the school.

547
00:34:06.725 --> 00:34:07.588
I'm saying first . . .

548
00:34:07.588 --> 00:34:09.025
<v MCKEE>All the way back to . . .

549
00:34:09.025 --> 00:34:11.325
<v GORDWIN>The boundary of the school to Perkin Town.

550
00:34:11.325 --> 00:34:13.338
<v MCKEE>How far west back to Perkins Town?

551
00:34:13.338 --> 00:34:15.062
<v CRAMER>And by the way that's Kim.

552
00:34:15.062 --> 00:34:16.788
Just . . . I'm talking to the microphone.

553
00:34:16.788 --> 00:34:17.075
<v MCKEE>Right.

554
00:34:17.075 --> 00:34:20.238
<v CRAMER>So when people transcribe this they'll know what to put there.

555
00:34:20.238 --> 00:34:20.525
<v MCKEE>Right.

556
00:34:20.525 --> 00:34:23.975
<v CRAMER>What is your . . . and is your last name Gordwin as well, or . . .?

557
00:34:23.975 --> 00:34:25.700
<v MCKEE>Yes, I was born a Gordwin.

558
00:34:25.700 --> 00:34:25.988
<v CRAMER>Okay.

559
00:34:25.988 --> 00:34:28.000
<v C. GORDWIN>Your last name is McKee now.

560
00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:31.222
<v CRAMER>Okay so your maiden name is Gordwin?

561
00:34:31.222 --> 00:34:31.683
<v MCKEE>[Agrees]

562
00:34:31.683 --> 00:34:32.143
<v CRAMER>Okay.

563
00:34:32.143 --> 00:34:37.667
Very good so that'll be . . . We'll just have that for the record.

564
00:34:37.667 --> 00:34:39.968
<v GORDWIN>Yes that's my oldest daughter.

565
00:34:39.968 --> 00:34:40.429
<v CRAMER>Okay.

566
00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:47.333
So who were the most important people in your community when you were growing up?

567
00:34:47.333 --> 00:34:53.317
<v GORDWIN>Mr. Josh Rigmaiden, and Mr. Audrey Prater, and I would say my mother.

568
00:34:53.317 --> 00:34:53.778
<v CRAMER>Why?

569
00:34:53.778 --> 00:34:57.000
What was . . . What about these people?

570
00:34:57.000 --> 00:34:59.901
<v GORDWIN>Mr. Josh Rigmaiden always . . . He had transportation.

571
00:34:59.901 --> 00:35:01.559
He had a car.

572
00:35:01.559 --> 00:35:06.533
My daddy had a car, and Mr. Audrey Prater had a car.

573
00:35:06.533 --> 00:35:10.263
Mr. Audrey Prater was with the sheriff's department always.

574
00:35:10.263 --> 00:35:14.822
Mr. Josh was always with the other part of the government.

575
00:35:14.822 --> 00:35:16.066
Like with the . . .

576
00:35:16.066 --> 00:35:19.796
<v MCKEE>Has to say it’s like what Hal McMillin is . . .

577
00:35:19.796 --> 00:35:21.868
<v GORDWIN>. . . McMillin is . . . Yeah Mr. Hal.

578
00:35:21.868 --> 00:35:22.697
Police juryman.

579
00:35:22.697 --> 00:35:26.013
<v MCKEE>Police juryman . . . Go and speak for the community . . .

580
00:35:26.013 --> 00:35:28.086
<v GORDWIN>Yes he was with that.

581
00:35:28.086 --> 00:35:30.987
<v MCKEE>For the black community back then, Jennifer

582
00:35:30.987 --> 00:35:37.204
<v GORDWIN>They went to . . . they met in . . . When it was time to vote in the ’50s.

583
00:35:37.204 --> 00:35:48.395
It was a man lived on [Highway] 90 that . . . a white man that came and mister . . . That would always go around and tell everybody they could vote.

584
00:35:48.395 --> 00:35:50.882
You have your rights to vote.

585
00:35:50.882 --> 00:35:55.855
He would take them up to register to vote in the ’50s.

586
00:35:55.855 --> 00:36:00.000
<v CRAMER>Do you remember who that was by any chance?

587
00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:02.441
<v GORDWIN>That was one of those [fugileirs?].

588
00:36:02.441 --> 00:36:02.847
<v CRAMER>Okay.

589
00:36:02.847 --> 00:36:08.136
<v GORDWIN>He was from down around Opelousas and he had business places on 90.

590
00:36:08.136 --> 00:36:10.169
<v CRAMER>That was in the ’50s?

591
00:36:10.169 --> 00:36:11.797
<v GORDWIN>In the ’50s, yes.

592
00:36:11.797 --> 00:36:13.017
In the ’50s.

593
00:36:13.017 --> 00:36:24.000
<v MCKEE>When you was coming up as a girl Mama the most important peoples was Mr. Josh Rigmaiden, Mr. Prater, and you said your mother Mrs. Edward.

594
00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:26.183
<v GORDWIN>Yeah my mother, Ida Mae.

595
00:36:26.183 --> 00:36:27.492
<v MCKEE>Ida Mae Lemelle.

596
00:36:27.492 --> 00:36:29.238
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees] And my grandmother.

597
00:36:29.238 --> 00:36:30.984
<v MCKEE>Well say her name.

598
00:36:30.984 --> 00:36:32.294
<v GORDWIN>Irie Viola Towner.

599
00:36:32.294 --> 00:36:34.040
<v MCKEE>And your mother's name.

600
00:36:34.040 --> 00:36:35.349
<v GORDWIN>Ida Mae Lemelle.

601
00:36:35.349 --> 00:36:38.841
<v CRAMER>So what was it that made your parents . . .?

602
00:36:38.841 --> 00:36:48.008
<v GORDWIN>My mother . . . My daddy didn't do a lot of things in the community because he was at work all the time.

