<v GORDWIN>Yes.

666
00:39:54.853 --> 00:39:55.824
<v CRAMER>Took some pictures.

667
00:39:55.824 --> 00:39:57.765
<v GORDWIN>We all went to church there.

668
00:39:57.765 --> 00:39:58.412
My family.

669
00:39:58.412 --> 00:40:01.000
<v CRAMER>Who were the leaders in your church?

670
00:40:01.000 --> 00:40:02.494
<v GORDWIN>When I was growing up . . .

671
00:40:02.494 --> 00:40:03.391
<v MCKEE>Not to interrupt . . .

672
00:40:03.391 --> 00:40:03.989
<v CRAMER>Yeah, sure.

673
00:40:03.989 --> 00:40:06.080
<v MCKEE>As a child who was her leader?

674
00:40:06.080 --> 00:40:08.172
As she got older they changed ministers.

675
00:40:08.172 --> 00:40:11.460
So you wanted when she was growing up as a child?

676
00:40:11.460 --> 00:40:12.954
<v CRAMER>I would say over time.

677
00:40:12.954 --> 00:40:17.138
So maybe somebody who was a leader in the ’40s and then maybe somebody . . .

678
00:40:17.138 --> 00:40:19.529
<v GORDWIN>I can tell you who I can remember.

679
00:40:19.529 --> 00:40:21.023
Reverend [Cuban?] from Lake Charles.

680
00:40:21.023 --> 00:40:25.207
I don't know where he was from, but he lived in Lake Charles then.

681
00:40:25.207 --> 00:40:27.000
<v CRAMER>How do you spell that?

682
00:40:27.000 --> 00:40:28.756
<v GORDWIN>I don't know.

683
00:40:28.756 --> 00:40:35.778
I was too little to spell, but it's on record at church.

684
00:40:35.778 --> 00:40:36.364
<v CRAMER>Okay.

685
00:40:36.364 --> 00:40:36.949
<v GORDWIN>Reverend.

686
00:40:36.949 --> 00:40:38.119
<v C. GORDWIN>[. . .?] right here.

687
00:40:38.119 --> 00:40:39.290
<v CRAMER>Okay, great.

688
00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:40.460
Thank you.

689
00:40:40.460 --> 00:40:48.653
<v GORDWIN>Reverend [J.O.?] Simmons, which my sister married Reverend Simmons' son, one of my sisters,

690
00:40:48.653 --> 00:40:56.261
and she died very early when she gave birth to her second baby.

691
00:40:56.261 --> 00:41:00.358
Her name was Pearly Mae Lemelle Simmons.

692
00:41:00.358 --> 00:41:03.284
That was Mother's third daughter.

693
00:41:03.284 --> 00:41:04.455
Reverend R.L.

694
00:41:04.455 --> 00:41:13.818
White, I can remember as I was quite a teenager then, when Reverend White was pastor.

695
00:41:13.818 --> 00:41:16.744
I was married too then.

696
00:41:16.744 --> 00:41:17.915
I married.

697
00:41:17.915 --> 00:41:20.256
I was married then.

698
00:41:20.256 --> 00:41:25.523
He was there when Grandmother and Grandpa got married.

699
00:41:25.523 --> 00:41:33.716
Their fiftieth anniversary in 1950 because Grandpa Towner and Grandma Towner married in 1900.

700
00:41:33.716 --> 00:41:35.472
He was there.

701
00:41:35.472 --> 00:41:37.227
He married them.

702
00:41:37.227 --> 00:41:40.739
I have a picture of them.

703
00:41:40.739 --> 00:41:46.006
And Reverend Taylor . . . after Reverend White died, Reverend Taylor.

704
00:41:46.006 --> 00:41:52.443
Then Reverend Achan which is still pastor, but he's ill now.

705
00:41:52.443 --> 00:42:03.562
He's not very active but they won't fire him because he says he's trying still going to be well,

706
00:42:03.562 --> 00:42:07.074
to be . . . But he was down.

707
00:42:07.074 --> 00:42:16.783
I don't think he's . . . And I haven’t been going to church a plenty now because with my condition.

708
00:42:16.783 --> 00:42:24.050
I can't stand to be around a lot of people with coughing, allergies and things.

709
00:42:24.050 --> 00:42:25.988
Different colognes and stuff.

710
00:42:25.988 --> 00:42:34.224
So I think I would just be an interruption at church, so I kind of stay home.

711
00:42:34.224 --> 00:42:45.366
But I take in my . . . reading my bible and listening to spirituals and the kids bring me different spiritual materials in to read.

712
00:42:45.366 --> 00:42:51.180
So then Reverend McKeever Edwards was our Sunday school superintendent for years.

713
00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:59.901
Let's see, my brother-in-law, which was Lorenzo Vincent, he was head of the deacon board at one time.

714
00:42:59.901 --> 00:43:11.043
He died holding that position, but I think he gave it up after he knew that he was really sick and couldn't go.

715
00:43:11.043 --> 00:43:23.640
It had different musicians from different places, from Lake Charles, and we had one musician from here that was [Ivy?] Vincent that was Uncle [Brack's?] brother.

716
00:43:23.640 --> 00:43:28.000
He was our musician one and he died.

717
00:43:28.000 --> 00:43:29.217
<v MCKEE>That was Mr. Lorenzo.

718
00:43:29.217 --> 00:43:30.435
Vincent, her brother-in-law's brother.

719
00:43:30.435 --> 00:43:30.739
<v CRAMER>Okay.

720
00:43:30.739 --> 00:43:31.957
What did he play?

721
00:43:31.957 --> 00:43:32.261
<v GORDWIN>Piano.

722
00:43:32.261 --> 00:43:32.565
<v CRAMER>Okay.

723
00:43:32.565 --> 00:43:34.087
Where you in the choir?

724
00:43:34.087 --> 00:43:35.000
Or was it [?]?

725
00:43:35.000 --> 00:43:37.130
<v GORDWIN>Yes, I was in the junior choir.

726
00:43:37.130 --> 00:43:37.435
<v CRAMER>Okay.

727
00:43:37.435 --> 00:43:39.565
<v GORDWIN>I was in the senior choir, too.

728
00:43:39.565 --> 00:43:42.000
<v C. GORDWIN>She was our youth leader at one time.

729
00:43:42.000 --> 00:43:44.130
<v CRAMER>I was going to ask about that.

730
00:43:44.130 --> 00:43:49.000
So what kind of . . . Did you have a youth group when you were growing up?

731
00:43:49.000 --> 00:43:50.600
<v GORDWIN>Yeah over the choir.

732
00:43:50.600 --> 00:43:53.000
Yes I had a youth group.

733
00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:55.000
It was a Sunshine Band.

734
00:43:55.000 --> 00:43:58.200
Ms. Ella Moss was one of our leaders.

735
00:43:58.200 --> 00:44:12.200
Ms. Thelma Edwards was one our leaders and her babies were . . . She had two little girls and I remember [by?] we couldn't get to the church we would go to her house for our meetings.

736
00:44:12.200 --> 00:44:15.000
She was always a very nice lady.

737
00:44:15.000 --> 00:44:15.800
Very clean.

738
00:44:15.800 --> 00:44:20.200
Her house spotless clean and her little kids well-groomed and everything.

739
00:44:20.200 --> 00:44:21.400
All the time.

740
00:44:21.400 --> 00:44:24.200
<v MCKEE>Which was . . . That was McKeever Edward’s wife.

741
00:44:24.200 --> 00:44:25.000
<v GORDWIN>Edward’s Wife.

742
00:44:25.000 --> 00:44:29.000
<v MCKEE>That she talked about that was a Sunday school teacher.

743
00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:29.400
<v GORDWIN>Wife.

744
00:44:29.400 --> 00:44:32.200
And he was in the army then.

745
00:44:32.200 --> 00:44:36.600
He was off to war then when she was our leader.

746
00:44:36.600 --> 00:44:39.800
Ms. Ava Prater was over our youth department.

747
00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:44.600
Ava Prater which was a person . . . Being here, she was a Johnson.

748
00:44:44.600 --> 00:44:47.000
She was born a Johnson.

749
00:44:47.000 --> 00:44:52.403
<v CRAMER>So not to . . . I'm really always curious about . . . and sorry to skip around a little bit,

750
00:44:52.403 --> 00:44:56.117
but I'm really curious about how the war affected your community.

751
00:44:56.117 --> 00:44:58.143
So when people went to war . . .

752
00:44:58.143 --> 00:44:59.156
<v GORDWIN>And came back.

753
00:44:59.156 --> 00:45:04.221
<v CRAMER>Maybe they didn’t . . . Maybe some people didn't come back, but when they did come back . . .

754
00:45:04.221 --> 00:45:06.247
<v GORDWIN>But thank God everybody came back.

755
00:45:06.247 --> 00:45:06.922
<v CRAMER>They did?

756
00:45:06.922 --> 00:45:08.273
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees] Everybody came back.

757
00:45:08.273 --> 00:45:08.948
<v CRAMER>That's amazing.

758
00:45:08.948 --> 00:45:13.000
<v GORDWIN>With no bad wounds that show, but inside . . . in the mind.

759
00:45:13.000 --> 00:45:23.347
<v CRAMER>What kind of effect did . . . What kind of changes did people who had been out and traveled abroad coming back to Mossville and coming back to Louisiana . . .

760
00:45:23.347 --> 00:45:27.179
<v GORDWIN>Well when they came back the refinery had started up.

761
00:45:27.179 --> 00:45:27.562
<v CRAMER>Interesting.

762
00:45:27.562 --> 00:45:28.328
<v GORDWIN>In '42.

763
00:45:28.328 --> 00:45:35.226
So they . . . everybody . . . If you went to service you had a choice at a job at the refinery.

764
00:45:35.226 --> 00:45:37.142
They would hire them first.

765
00:45:37.142 --> 00:45:39.058
My brother-in-law, Richard Harrison Lee,

766
00:45:39.058 --> 00:45:50.555
that married my sister which it was an arranged marriage while he was in the war and she was in high school for them to marry when he get home.

767
00:45:50.555 --> 00:45:52.471
My daddy and his daddy.

768
00:45:52.471 --> 00:45:55.536
Jim Lee and Edward Lemelle arranged the marriage.

769
00:45:55.536 --> 00:46:01.668
He told Dad he wanted his son to marry the daughter and they did just that.

770
00:46:01.668 --> 00:46:05.117
When they came back they had a big wedding.

771
00:46:05.117 --> 00:46:07.033
I mean a big wedding.

772
00:46:07.033 --> 00:46:09.715
<v MCKEE>He came home with a purple heart.

773
00:46:09.715 --> 00:46:10.099
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

774
00:46:10.099 --> 00:46:10.865
Richard did.

775
00:46:10.865 --> 00:46:15.464
He was the only one that got . . . like a medal like that.

776
00:46:15.464 --> 00:46:17.380
<c.p>He saved a whole platoon.

777
00:46:17.380 --> 00:46:23.894
He had a big burn on his hand I remember from it where he got wounded at.

778
00:46:23.894 --> 00:46:25.044
He still worked.

779
00:46:25.044 --> 00:46:27.343
He worked at Cities Service then.

780
00:46:27.343 --> 00:46:33.091
Then he . . . After he retired from there he went to work at an animal clinic.

781
00:46:33.091 --> 00:46:38.073
He used to bring me a cat always, which my kids hated cats.

782
00:46:38.073 --> 00:46:47.270
He would see that they were . . . had everything like watched the ringworms on them and everything and bring me a cat all the time.

783
00:46:47.270 --> 00:46:49.186
And I loved the cats.

784
00:46:49.186 --> 00:46:50.336
I love cats.

785
00:46:50.336 --> 00:46:53.018
I had one when I was little.

786
00:46:53.018 --> 00:46:58.000
Daddy didn't let us have dogs because they'd tear up your flowerbeds.

787
00:46:58.000 --> 00:47:01.879
<v CRAMER>But when you were growing up though, you guys had animals, right?

788
00:47:01.879 --> 00:47:04.465
So you guys had livestock, and chickens, and . . .

789
00:47:04.465 --> 00:47:05.758
<v GORDWIN>Oh we had . . . Yeah.

790
00:47:05.758 --> 00:47:06.727
Horses, cows, everything.

791
00:47:06.727 --> 00:47:07.051
Hogs.

792
00:47:07.051 --> 00:47:08.990
We would butcher hog every fall.

793
00:47:08.990 --> 00:47:13.838
Three or four hogs and Mother would give away a whole hog to the community.

794
00:47:13.838 --> 00:47:14.808
Everybody around us.

795
00:47:14.808 --> 00:47:17.394
<v MCKEE>Even though her father didn't want her to.

796
00:47:17.394 --> 00:47:18.687
She would do it.

797
00:47:18.687 --> 00:47:19.333
<v GORDWIN>Mother would.

798
00:47:19.333 --> 00:47:21.596
Mother would do what she wanted to.

799
00:47:21.596 --> 00:47:23.212
Daddy didn't mess with Mother.

800
00:47:23.212 --> 00:47:26.121
He let her do what she wanted to do.

801
00:47:26.121 --> 00:47:27.737
She always gave, gave, gave.

802
00:47:27.737 --> 00:47:30.646
And funny, back to me I give, give too.

803
00:47:30.646 --> 00:47:36.788
My son used to tell them, "Y'all can just stand here and listen to Mama if you want to,

804
00:47:36.788 --> 00:47:39.374
but I'm not giving all my stuff away.

805
00:47:39.374 --> 00:47:44.222
You around here and be poor if you listen to Mama with that charity stuff."

806
00:47:44.222 --> 00:47:49.394
But that's what I was taught too that you share what you have with other people.

807
00:47:49.394 --> 00:47:55.535
Then World War Two was going on I remember we could . . . We had money, but we couldn't buy things.

808
00:47:55.535 --> 00:47:57.152
They was rationing the food.

809
00:47:57.152 --> 00:48:00.384
We had a little token we had to give some

810
00:48:00.384 --> 00:48:02.000
<c.p>stamps to get stuff.

811
00:48:02.000 --> 00:48:05.186
<v CRAMER>Like tires, and sugar, and that kind of thing, right?

812
00:48:05.186 --> 00:48:05.823
<v GORDWIN>That's right.

813
00:48:05.823 --> 00:48:12.513
The people had a store in Sulphur that we could get . . . The man would give us more than our allowance was.

814
00:48:12.513 --> 00:48:13.788
A store in Sulphur.

815
00:48:13.788 --> 00:48:16.336
I think it was the [Misses?] I'm guessing.

816
00:48:16.336 --> 00:48:18.566
I don't know who they was then.

817
00:48:18.566 --> 00:48:22.708
I don't know their names then, but everybody knew where you could go.

818
00:48:22.708 --> 00:48:29.080
Well a few people knew where you could go get . . . And they would go and get it for you, so.

819
00:48:29.080 --> 00:48:36.088
<v MCKEE>Tell her about when you was a little girl Mama and you would . . . Your mom would be out in the yard working.

820
00:48:36.088 --> 00:48:38.000
<v GORDWIN>We done talked about that.

821
00:48:38.000 --> 00:48:39.351
<v MCKEE>About the . . . Oh y'all did?

822
00:48:39.351 --> 00:48:39.892
<v CRAMER>We did.

823
00:48:39.892 --> 00:48:40.162
Yes.

824
00:48:40.162 --> 00:48:42.595
<v MCKEE>Oh she told y'all about the wheelbarrow and stuff?

825
00:48:42.595 --> 00:48:42.865
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees]

826
00:48:42.865 --> 00:48:46.919
<v CRAMER>Yes, yes and actually if you don't mind I'd love to hear about the garden.

827
00:48:46.919 --> 00:48:47.459
<v GORDWIN>The garden?

828
00:48:47.459 --> 00:48:48.000
<v CRAMER>Yes.

829
00:48:48.000 --> 00:48:51.575
<v GORDWIN>Daddy did all the hard work in the garden.

830
00:48:51.575 --> 00:48:57.932
We could help him like when he would . . . Mother would gather the vegetables, and Daddy planted.

831
00:48:57.932 --> 00:49:02.301
He didn't want us to hoe and all this heavy work.

832
00:49:02.301 --> 00:49:05.479
He didn't want his girls to do that.

833
00:49:05.479 --> 00:49:08.260
He would cut the wood at night.

834
00:49:08.260 --> 00:49:16.205
We would have to bring it in the next day and stack it on the porch where we kept it.

835
00:49:16.205 --> 00:49:20.973
Mother always gathered the vegetables from the garden and I followed her.

836
00:49:20.973 --> 00:49:26.534
Daddy plant . . . In the fall we planted mustard greens, turnip greens, cabbage, cauliflower, broccoli.

837
00:49:26.534 --> 00:49:26.932
Everything.

838
00:49:26.932 --> 00:49:28.521
All vegetables we had.

839
00:49:28.521 --> 00:49:29.712
<v CRAMER>What about summer?

840
00:49:29.712 --> 00:49:38.452
<v GORDWIN>In the summer we had string beans, butter beans . . . And the butter beans would always last until the fall of the year.

841
00:49:38.452 --> 00:49:47.986
If you did it right . . . Daddy always did the string . . . The ones that run on the pole and he never planted bunch string beans.

842
00:49:47.986 --> 00:49:50.767
He always ran long Kentucky Wonder beans.

843
00:49:50.767 --> 00:49:51.959
They were good.

844
00:49:51.959 --> 00:50:01.890
Sometime Mother would pick them before they get fully grown and they would be so tender and sweet she would cook them whole like that.

845
00:50:01.890 --> 00:50:04.671
Kind of string them down the back.

846
00:50:04.671 --> 00:50:09.438
The say string the beans get the little stem out of them.

847
00:50:09.438 --> 00:50:11.425
We had corn, field peas . . .

848
00:50:11.425 --> 00:50:11.822
<v CHARLES GORDWIN>Tomatoes.

849
00:50:11.822 --> 00:50:12.616
<v GORDWIN>. . . tomatoes, carrots . . .

850
00:50:12.616 --> 00:50:13.411
<c.p>MCKEE : Okra.

851
00:50:13.411 --> 00:50:15.000
<v GORDWIN>. . . strawberries, okra, everything.

852
00:50:15.000 --> 00:50:16.154
<v CRAMER>What about peanuts?

853
00:50:16.154 --> 00:50:17.692
Did peanuts grow there?

854
00:50:17.692 --> 00:50:24.615
<v GORDWIN>Yes, we did peanuts and we did . . . Daddy even planted corn that you could pop . . . Popcorn and everything.

855
00:50:24.615 --> 00:50:25.385
<v CRAMER>Oh, wow.

856
00:50:25.385 --> 00:50:26.538
What about fruit?

857
00:50:26.538 --> 00:50:28.462
Did you have any trees?

858
00:50:28.462 --> 00:50:30.000
<v GORDWIN>Yes, we had fruit.

859
00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:31.154
<v CH. GORDWIN>Figs, pears, plums.

860
00:50:31.154 --> 00:50:32.692
<v GORDWIN>Figs, pears, and oranges.

861
00:50:32.692 --> 00:50:33.462
<v CH. GORDWIN>And plums.

862
00:50:33.462 --> 00:50:35.000
<v GORDWIN>And plums, yes.

863
00:50:35.000 --> 00:50:37.498
<v CRAMER>So what about preserving, any of that?

864
00:50:37.498 --> 00:50:37.855
Canning?

865
00:50:37.855 --> 00:50:44.991
<v GORDWIN>Mother used to preserve every year and we'd go in the woods and pick blackberries and we would pick mayhaws.

866
00:50:44.991 --> 00:50:47.846
She made mayhaw jelly and the blackberry jelly.

867
00:50:47.846 --> 00:50:52.128
<v CRAMER>What were your chores when you were growing up as far as . . .?

868
00:50:52.128 --> 00:50:52.485
<v GORDWIN>Eating.

869
00:50:52.485 --> 00:50:53.555
<v C. GORDWIN>No, she babysat.

870
00:50:53.555 --> 00:50:57.480
<v CRAMER>What about . . . I'm sure you worked in the yard and cleaned.

871
00:50:57.480 --> 00:50:58.551
<v MCKEE>She had chores.

872
00:50:58.551 --> 00:50:59.264
She cleaned.

873
00:50:59.264 --> 00:51:00.692
<v GORDWIN>Well no, right . . . really?

874
00:51:00.692 --> 00:51:02.476
To tell you the truth?

875
00:51:02.476 --> 00:51:02.833
<v CRAMER>Yeah.

876
00:51:02.833 --> 00:51:03.189
Yeah.

877
00:51:03.189 --> 00:51:06.758
<v GORDWIN>You see I had the four sisters older than me.

878
00:51:06.758 --> 00:51:09.612
They did everything when I was growing up.

879
00:51:09.612 --> 00:51:14.965
So after I got . . . I was almost grown when I had the responsibility of cooking,

880
00:51:14.965 --> 00:51:18.176
<c.p>and washing dishes, and helping with the . . . at home.

881
00:51:18.176 --> 00:51:18.890
But I . . .

882
00:51:18.890 --> 00:51:20.674
<v CH. GORDWIN>Babysitting your nieces and nephews . . .

883
00:51:20.674 --> 00:51:24.956
<v GORDWIN>Mother . . . Yeah I would babysit a lot after my sisters had babies.

884
00:51:24.956 --> 00:51:26.026
I would babysit.

885
00:51:26.026 --> 00:51:32.806
Then I would go out and work in Maplewood and babysit for ladies that Mother knew with they children.

886
00:51:32.806 --> 00:51:36.374
And I would . . . We would walk to work in Maplewood,

887
00:51:36.374 --> 00:51:42.441
from where I lived to there, and we all bunch of us get together and all walk.

888
00:51:42.441 --> 00:51:48.150
Everybody go their little way and then we'd meet up that evening and come back home,

889
00:51:48.150 --> 00:51:51.718
but . . . and we were making . . . Earning fifty cents an hour.

890
00:51:51.718 --> 00:51:53.145
Can you believe it?

891
00:51:53.145 --> 00:51:54.573
Fifty cents an hour.

892
00:51:54.573 --> 00:51:56.000
<v CRAMER>When was that?

893
00:51:56.000 --> 00:51:59.515
<v GORDWIN>That was back in the ’40s and ’50s.

894
00:51:59.515 --> 00:52:07.864
We worked ironing the clothes, these khaki uniforms these operators wore, and scrubbing floors, cleaning these waxed hardwood floors.

895
00:52:07.864 --> 00:52:08.742
Doing that.

896
00:52:08.742 --> 00:52:10.061
Waxing the floors.

897
00:52:10.061 --> 00:52:14.015
We always walked to work and walked back home.

898
00:52:14.015 --> 00:52:19.727
Now the kids can ride to work and they don't want to go.

899
00:52:19.727 --> 00:52:21.485
<v CRAMER>How far was that?

900
00:52:21.485 --> 00:52:23.682
<v GORDWIN>About two miles I guess.

901
00:52:23.682 --> 00:52:25.879
<v CH. GORDWIN>From Old Spanish Trail back [ . . .?].

902
00:52:25.879 --> 00:52:27.197
<v GORDWIN>Passing across 90.

903
00:52:27.197 --> 00:52:31.591
Give me some water somebody. [Coughs] Bring my Kleenex, too.

904
00:52:31.591 --> 00:52:33.788
<v CRAMER>Pause it [break in tape].

905
00:52:33.788 --> 00:52:36.424
<v CH. GORDWIN>[Pretty much?] I mean I was . . .

906
00:52:36.424 --> 00:52:38.621
<v MCKEE>You ready to start back?

907
00:52:38.621 --> 00:52:47.409
<v CRAMER>Yeah I wanted to record this about revivals and curing and of . . . because I wanted to ask questions about revivals.

908
00:52:47.409 --> 00:52:52.682
There's so many questions I want to ask as you can tell.

909
00:52:52.682 --> 00:52:54.000
<v GORDWIN>Go ahead.

910
00:52:54.000 --> 00:53:00.671
<v CRAMER>But we were talking off tape about asthma and how Charles . . . Do you mind talking about that?

911
00:53:00.671 --> 00:53:01.848
<v CH. GORDWIN>Oh, no, no.

912
00:53:01.848 --> 00:53:07.342
<v CRAMER>About how you were healed, and then you started talking about a revival, so.

913
00:53:07.342 --> 00:53:12.835
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yeah it was when I was a kid I used to suffer from asthma.

914
00:53:12.835 --> 00:53:13.620
In the

915
00:53:13.620 --> 00:53:15.582
<c.p>wintertime it was the worst.

916
00:53:15.582 --> 00:53:18.722
Mom used to cook special meals for me.

917
00:53:18.722 --> 00:53:21.076
Everybody got to eat regular food.

918
00:53:21.076 --> 00:53:24.215
All I could eat was like starchy foods.

919
00:53:24.215 --> 00:53:26.962
Oatmeal, and barley, and stuff and potatoes.

920
00:53:26.962 --> 00:53:32.848
Various meats I could eat, beef, and because of the cows and chickens had fur.

921
00:53:32.848 --> 00:53:37.949
The cows had fur and the chickens had feathers and I was allergic.

922
00:53:37.949 --> 00:53:39.911
I had allergies and asthma.

923
00:53:39.911 --> 00:53:44.228
So just things I couldn't eat and I couldn't drink milk.

924
00:53:44.228 --> 00:53:46.582
It's just a lot of stuff.

925
00:53:46.582 --> 00:53:48.152
I couldn't do bread.

926
00:53:48.152 --> 00:53:49.329
Couldn't eat bread.

927
00:53:49.329 --> 00:53:50.114
Anything wheat.

928
00:53:50.114 --> 00:53:52.076
<v CRAMER>So what could you eat?

929
00:53:52.076 --> 00:53:52.468
Vegetables?

930
00:53:52.468 --> 00:53:53.646
<v CH. GORDWIN>Just starchy foods.

931
00:53:53.646 --> 00:53:55.215
Vegetables and like potatoes.

932
00:53:55.215 --> 00:53:56.000
<v CRAMER>Fish?

933
00:53:56.000 --> 00:53:56.343
<v CH. GORDWIN>Fish.

934
00:53:56.343 --> 00:53:58.404
We did a lot of fish.

935
00:53:58.404 --> 00:54:01.496
My mom just start cooking like that for everybody.

936
00:54:01.496 --> 00:54:14.891
She used to . . . The rest of the kids say it wasn't fair so she would buy the Hostess Ding Dong . . .The Hostess Twinkies and the Ding Dongs and she'd put them up and they knew where they was at.

937
00:54:14.891 --> 00:54:18.326
I start watching them where they was hiding them at.

938
00:54:18.326 --> 00:54:22.448
So being that I was my mother's child, I was greedy also.

939
00:54:22.448 --> 00:54:32.409
So I used to go find them and eat them and boom there go the asthma attack because I couldn't eat the chocolate and I'd have an asthma attack.

940
00:54:32.409 --> 00:54:35.500
They'd go . . . Mama, "Why'd y'all let him eat it?"

941
00:54:35.500 --> 00:54:37.561
"We didn't feed it to him.

942
00:54:37.561 --> 00:54:39.278
He went and found them."

943
00:54:39.278 --> 00:54:42.026
Then I start keeping to a strict diet.

944
00:54:42.026 --> 00:54:46.491
She used to drive me to Beaumont [Texas] to a doctor for allergies.

945
00:54:46.491 --> 00:54:50.270
I used to have to take a shot once a week.

946
00:54:50.270 --> 00:54:53.017
When I started . . . When I got old enough

947
00:54:53.017 --> 00:54:59.887
<c.p>and thought I was grown I started smoking and she jammed me up against the wall and start punching me.

948
00:54:59.887 --> 00:55:08.130
She said, "All that money I used to spend on you going back and forth to Beaumont and now you want to smoke cigarettes."

949
00:55:08.130 --> 00:55:10.191
Because I couldn't even stand smoke.

950
00:55:10.191 --> 00:55:11.222
Couldn't be outside.

951
00:55:11.222 --> 00:55:13.626
Couldn't eat barbeque because of the smoke.

952
00:55:13.626 --> 00:55:15.000
<v C. GORDWIN>Couldn't have firecrackers.

953
00:55:15.000 --> 00:55:22.952
<v CH. GORDWIN>Then I couldn't have firecrackers but I'd go . . . my stepdad used to take me and go and we'd buy . . . He'd buy me a hundred fifty dollars’ worth of firecrackers.

954
00:55:22.952 --> 00:55:26.242
Back then that was like three garbage . . . I mean three paper bags.

955
00:55:26.242 --> 00:55:27.887
That's what you had back then.

956
00:55:27.887 --> 00:55:30.903
And it’s just a lot of things that I was prohibited.

957
00:55:30.903 --> 00:55:32.000
I couldn't play football.

958
00:55:32.000 --> 00:55:36.113
Couldn't play any sports because I couldn't run because of the asthma and the allergies.

959
00:55:36.113 --> 00:55:39.129
I was allergic to grass but I stayed in the woods.

960
00:55:39.129 --> 00:55:41.597
Mom used to have to come holler for me.

961
00:55:41.597 --> 00:55:43.242
I'd stay out in the woods.

962
00:55:43.242 --> 00:55:45.161
<v CRAMER>Well that was a lot of restrictions.

963
00:55:45.161 --> 00:55:45.435
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yes.

964
00:55:45.435 --> 00:55:47.629
<v CRAMER>And mostly like what you wanted to do.

965
00:55:47.629 --> 00:55:48.452
Boy things, right?

966
00:55:48.452 --> 00:55:49.000
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yes.

967
00:55:49.000 --> 00:55:51.848
<v CRAMER>Well, so you talked about a revival.

968
00:55:51.848 --> 00:55:52.662
<v CH. GORDWIN>The revivals?

969
00:55:52.662 --> 00:55:53.069
<v CRAMER>Yes.

970
00:55:53.069 --> 00:56:02.021
<v CH. GORDWIN>And bringing, leading up that is I was . . . My mom was just getting tired of dealing with the asthma and the allergies.

971
00:56:02.021 --> 00:56:07.717
I'm sure she was, like I said, growing tired, but she never gave up.

972
00:56:07.717 --> 00:56:20.331
She always . . . So when she heard that they had a revival on the corner she took me to the . . . Walked over there and she asked the man to pray for me.

973
00:56:20.331 --> 00:56:24.807
Asked for God to deliver me from that and he did.

974
00:56:24.807 --> 00:56:31.724
After that I didn't . . . But prior to that my uncle took me out in his front yard,

975
00:56:31.724 --> 00:56:40.269
my uncle Lorenzo Vincent Senior, he took me out in the front yard and he had a big live oak tree.

976
00:56:40.269 --> 00:56:48.000
It was surrounded . . . He had bricks surrounding it and I remember walking up there and it had like . . .

977
00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:52.969
<v GORDWIN>Didn't he say you get on the north side of the tree?

978
00:56:52.969 --> 00:56:55.039
Certain side of the tree?

979
00:56:55.039 --> 00:56:58.766
<v CH. GORDWIN>We’d get . . . I’ll just remember standing to this tree.

980
00:56:58.766 --> 00:57:06.219
I remember him taking some scissors and he clipped my hair and he bore a hole in it,

981
00:57:06.219 --> 00:57:11.602
and he stuck my hair in it and he capped it back off.

982
00:57:11.602 --> 00:57:19.883
Then from the old saying is that once the child passed that mark he would be cured of his illness.

983
00:57:19.883 --> 00:57:25.266
There's a lot of old remedies that we live by here in Mossville.

984
00:57:25.266 --> 00:57:32.719
My grandmother practiced a lot of them, and to this day we all still practice home remedies and

985
00:57:32.719 --> 00:57:34.375
<c.p>things of that nature.

986
00:57:34.375 --> 00:57:38.516
We . . . it was a liquid Lysol my grandmother . . . everything was . . .

987
00:57:38.516 --> 00:57:41.000
<v GORDWIN>It's killing me right now.

988
00:57:41.000 --> 00:57:49.143
<v CH. GORDWIN>Everything was . . . Liquid Lysol was a common thing in the house just like Clorox is today because it was a disinfectant.

989
00:57:49.143 --> 00:57:50.306
<v C. GORDWIN>Famous liquid Lysol.

990
00:57:50.306 --> 00:57:50.694
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yes.

991
00:57:50.694 --> 00:57:52.633
<v C. GORDWIN>It was hard to find.

992
00:57:52.633 --> 00:57:54.184
<v CH. GORDWIN>We lived by that.

993
00:57:54.184 --> 00:57:58.061
Clorox and liquid Lysol because my grandmother was so clean.

994
00:57:58.061 --> 00:58:00.000
Everything was cleaned and . . .

995
00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:01.682
<v CRAMER>So it cured you?

996
00:58:01.682 --> 00:58:02.103
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yes.

997
00:58:02.103 --> 00:58:06.308
<v CRAMER>The combination maybe of your revival and then the hair.

998
00:58:06.308 --> 00:58:06.729
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yeah.

999
00:58:06.729 --> 00:58:10.935
<v C. GORDWIN>I’m going to tell you one thing about the Clorox.

1000
00:58:10.935 --> 00:58:18.925
When I was working out west with the Clorox they would . . . This is when the rampant scare of HIV.

1001
00:58:18.925 --> 00:58:19.346
<v GORDWIN>Right.

1002
00:58:19.346 --> 00:58:31.542
<v C. GORDWIN>They were using a lot of pink solution that they were paying high price for to disinfect and make sure at a shelter situation that contamination and all this.

1003
00:58:31.542 --> 00:58:33.224
It was very expensive.

1004
00:58:33.224 --> 00:58:35.748
Gallon four hundred and some dollars.

1005
00:58:35.748 --> 00:58:42.477
Hundred and fifty or something like that to go behind and mop, and clean, and everything.

1006
00:58:42.477 --> 00:58:49.206
I was working phone lines in the office and they would wipe the phones with alcohol.

1007
00:58:49.206 --> 00:58:54.252
So when I would come in on my shift, Clorox and water.

1008
00:58:54.252 --> 00:58:57.196
They didn't have the Clorox wipes then.

1009
00:58:57.196 --> 00:59:00.140
I would wipe all the phones down.

1010
00:59:00.140 --> 00:59:06.028
All the phones and the bases and everything down with Clorox and water solution.

1011
00:59:06.028 --> 00:59:09.813
Mostly Clorox because my hands would be burning red.

1012
00:59:09.813 --> 00:59:14.019
So when other people would pick up . . ."Oh this smells.

1013
00:59:14.019 --> 00:59:15.280
Oh she's here."

1014
00:59:15.280 --> 00:59:16.963
They wouldn't see me.

1015
00:59:16.963 --> 00:59:19.907
"She's here goes the southern Clorox girl.

1016
00:59:19.907 --> 00:59:20.748
She's here."

1017
00:59:20.748 --> 00:59:27.477
Then after I worked there for a while, guess what's the universal solution for this virus?

1018
00:59:27.477 --> 00:59:27.897
Clorox.

1019
00:59:27.897 --> 00:59:30.000
<v CRAMER>Not the pink stuff?

1020
00:59:30.000 --> 00:59:31.700
<v C. GORDWIN>Not the pink stuff.

1021
00:59:31.700 --> 00:59:32.550
The Clorox.

1022
00:59:32.550 --> 00:59:33.400
Cheaper version.

1023
00:59:33.400 --> 00:59:33.825
<v CRAMER>Right.

1024
00:59:33.825 --> 00:59:34.250
Absolutely.

1025
00:59:34.250 --> 00:59:37.650
<v C. GORDWIN>So I say, "Talk about me now." [laughs]

1026
00:59:37.650 --> 00:59:39.775
<v CH. GORDWIN>So back to the revivals.

1027
00:59:39.775 --> 00:59:45.300
We . . . In the church we were born and raised in at Mount Zion.

1028
00:59:45.300 --> 00:59:48.700
My grandmother was known for the cooking lady.

1029
00:59:48.700 --> 00:59:51.250
She cooks well, great, everything superb.

1030
00:59:51.250 --> 00:59:55.925
Different flavor than everybody around in the surrounding area within Mossville.

1031
00:59:55.925 --> 00:59:59.750
When . . . This is from when I was a kid.

1032
00:59:59.750 --> 01:00:06.550
They would ask . . . I know they would ask my grandmother to come, I mean to cook.

1033
01:00:06.550 --> 01:00:20.150
And the men that was coming in for the revivals . . . Like we’d have a week of revival that would start like seven o'clock at night and probably let out around nine thirty.

1034
01:00:20.150 --> 01:00:23.125
My grandmother every night she was cook.

1035
01:00:23.125 --> 01:00:31.200
That whole day she’d cook the food for my grandfather and then she'd have her own other little cooking.

1036
01:00:31.200 --> 01:00:35.875
My grandmother spent ninety percent of her time in the kitchen.

1037
01:00:35.875 --> 01:00:38.000
<v CRAMER>This is Ida Mae?

1038
01:00:38.000 --> 01:00:39.630
<v CH. GORDWIN>Ms. Ida Mae Lemelle.

1039
01:00:39.630 --> 01:00:47.373
We'd be up there and it's just kids and we know it’s a big thing . . . The Evangelist is coming.

1040
01:00:47.373 --> 01:00:49.411
Coming to “Big Mama's” house.

1041
01:00:49.411 --> 01:00:50.226
She'd cook.

1042
01:00:50.226 --> 01:00:53.078
I mean she'd have everything laid out.

1043
01:00:53.078 --> 01:00:59.191
She'd go get her gold dishes; her gold trim dishes, and forks, and spoons out.

1044
01:00:59.191 --> 01:01:02.451
She'd set the table real pretty and fine.

1045
01:01:02.451 --> 01:01:12.639
And I'm running through the house and run out past through the kitchen and see what I could grab and run, hide and eat it.

1046
01:01:12.639 --> 01:01:15.900
Just waiting for all those people to come.

1047
01:01:15.900 --> 01:01:22.420
Every night . . . And then we'd go home, we’d come home get ready to go to church.

1048
01:01:22.420 --> 01:01:26.903
All load up in the car and we'd go to church.

1049
01:01:26.903 --> 01:01:36.683
We’d . . . The kids would have to sing and we'd have church service and go to Big Mama's house and all the people would come.

1050
01:01:36.683 --> 01:01:40.759
That was a big thing to me as a kid.

1051
01:01:40.759 --> 01:01:43.611
We were rooted and grounded in church.

1052
01:01:43.611 --> 01:01:52.577
I think that's what's brought us this far and that's what held us and kept us close is for the bonding the

1053
01:01:52.577 --> 01:01:53.799
<c.p>Bible speaks of.

1054
01:01:53.799 --> 01:01:57.875
Love, charity . . . If you don't have that you have nothing.

1055
01:01:57.875 --> 01:02:01.542
But that's what we was rooted and grounded in.

1056
01:02:01.542 --> 01:02:03.987
My grandmother instilled that in us.

1057
01:02:03.987 --> 01:02:07.248
Love, cleaning, cooking, eating, being prideful about yourself.

1058
01:02:07.248 --> 01:02:15.398
Not having pride, but be proud of who you are and present yourself well in the neighborhood and respect yourself.

1059
01:02:15.398 --> 01:02:20.696
My grandmother and my grandfather was a big, big influence on our family.

1060
01:02:20.696 --> 01:02:41.887
<v CRAMER>I remember when I was talking to Butch that day that y'all took me out and we were in the yard which would have been awesome to do an interview right then and there because he was saying something like . . . he said something like . . . I think he turned to Kim and said,

1061
01:02:41.887 --> 01:02:45.962
"How many children have been raised up in this house?"

1062
01:02:45.962 --> 01:02:48.000
<v CH. GORDWIN>Half of the community.

1063
01:02:48.000 --> 01:02:48.585
<v CRAMER>Yeah, exactly.

1064
01:02:48.585 --> 01:02:54.433
So I mean and he . . . You could see when he was looking out he was kind of looking and remembering.

1065
01:02:54.433 --> 01:02:55.018
<v CH. GORDWIN>And remembering.

1066
01:02:55.018 --> 01:02:55.310
Yeah.

1067
01:02:55.310 --> 01:03:00.573
<v CRAMER>The structures that were on the property, the people who came to visit and that sort of thing.

1068
01:03:00.573 --> 01:03:03.789
And our goal is to try and sort of recreate that.

1069
01:03:03.789 --> 01:03:04.082
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

1070
01:03:04.082 --> 01:03:05.251
<v CRAMER>. . . Through stories and everything.

1071
01:03:05.251 --> 01:03:09.053
I mean I'd love to . . . I know that the highway sounds and everything,

1072
01:03:09.053 --> 01:03:21.041
but at some point in time I would love to take a microphone and a recorder out there and sort of recreate that moment because you could see that he was remember a lot of things and he was telling stories.

1073
01:03:21.041 --> 01:03:27.181
Then you're telling . . . What you're saying is the same thing as what he was saying about how special that place is.

1074
01:03:27.181 --> 01:03:30.105
And ultimately that's kind of what we're going for here.

1075
01:03:30.105 --> 01:03:34.784
So I know I'm kind of jumping around from here to there with all these questions . . .

1076
01:03:34.784 --> 01:03:35.076
<v CH. GORDWIN>Correct.

1077
01:03:35.076 --> 01:03:37.415
<v CRAMER>But they'll all be . . . kind of come together.

1078
01:03:37.415 --> 01:03:38.000
<v C. GORDWIN>Right.

1079
01:03:38.000 --> 01:03:39.565
<v CRAMER>And we’ll come back for more.

1080
01:03:39.565 --> 01:03:39.826
<v C. GORDWIN>Yes.

1081
01:03:39.826 --> 01:03:42.696
<v CRAMER>And I'm wondering like we probably have about twenty minutes left.

1082
01:03:42.696 --> 01:03:44.000
But I was wondering . . .

1083
01:03:44.000 --> 01:03:44.960
<v CH. GORDWIN>That clock's wrong.

1084
01:03:44.960 --> 01:03:45.920
<v CRAMER>Oh is it?

1085
01:03:45.920 --> 01:03:46.240
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yeah.

1086
01:03:46.240 --> 01:03:46.560
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1087
01:03:46.560 --> 01:03:47.200
I was . . .

1088
01:03:47.200 --> 01:03:48.800
<v C. GORDWIN>It's three fifty right now.

1089
01:03:48.800 --> 01:03:49.440
<v CRAMER>Oh goodness.

1090
01:03:49.440 --> 01:03:52.320
Well then we have thirty minutes left that's perfect.

1091
01:03:52.320 --> 01:03:52.640
<v C. GORDWIN>Yeah.

1092
01:03:52.640 --> 01:03:52.960
<v CRAMER>Well . . .

1093
01:03:52.960 --> 01:03:54.880
<v CH. GORDWIN>She just looked at her watch.

1094
01:03:54.880 --> 01:03:55.200
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1095
01:03:55.200 --> 01:04:00.000
So I would love to . . . So were revivals part of your growing up, too?

1096
01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:00.353
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

1097
01:04:00.353 --> 01:04:00.706
<v CH. GORDWIN>Yes.

1098
01:04:00.706 --> 01:04:02.118
<v CRAMER>And where were they?

1099
01:04:02.118 --> 01:04:03.882
You said on the corner.

1100
01:04:03.882 --> 01:04:04.941
<v GORDWIN>At the church.

1101
01:04:04.941 --> 01:04:10.235
<v CH. GORDWIN>Well this . . . we had a . . . they had a traveling . . . A traveling people and they different . . .

1102
01:04:10.235 --> 01:04:10.588
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1103
01:04:10.588 --> 01:04:12.000
<v GORDWIN>He's still here.

1104
01:04:12.000 --> 01:04:12.529
<v CRAMER>Oh really?

1105
01:04:12.529 --> 01:04:12.794
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees]

1106
01:04:12.794 --> 01:04:13.588
<v CH. GORDWIN>That man, yes.

1107
01:04:13.588 --> 01:04:15.176
<v C. GORDWIN>His son cuts my mother's yard.

1108
01:04:15.176 --> 01:04:15.441
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

1109
01:04:15.441 --> 01:04:16.765
<v CRAMER>So who is this person?

