1325
01:12:20.750 --> 01:12:23.237
You can show a picture of it.

1326
01:12:23.237 --> 01:12:31.408
We have to go to where the trees . . . Now around here the refineries kill all our stuff the . . . But it's a little thing.

1327
01:12:31.408 --> 01:12:33.539
It look something like a fern.

1328
01:12:33.539 --> 01:12:35.671
It grow on the oak tree.

1329
01:12:35.671 --> 01:12:38.158
And it’s a . . . we call it . . . now,

1330
01:12:38.158 --> 01:12:43.487
the German . . . Some German people was in here when my grandmother, her sons were young.

1331
01:12:43.487 --> 01:12:46.684
I probably wasn't . . . I know I wasn't born then.

1332
01:12:46.684 --> 01:12:54.855
Grandmother, they were living up on the Houston River Road, she said that they were in the woods and a snake bit one.

1333
01:12:54.855 --> 01:13:05.513
And they brought him into the house quick as they could because Grandmother had already showed them how to tie a tourniquet if a snake bite you above the bite.

1334
01:13:05.513 --> 01:13:08.000
They did that and brought him in.

1335
01:13:08.000 --> 01:13:11.197
And just when they were coming in the house,

1336
01:13:11.197 --> 01:13:19.368
these German people was driving up in Grandmother's yard because it was lots of little long trail from the road to the house.

1337
01:13:19.368 --> 01:13:20.789
They say, "What's wrong?"

1338
01:13:20.789 --> 01:13:21.855
They said, "Snake.

1339
01:13:21.855 --> 01:13:25.053
He got snake . . ." And the man said, "Oh look!

1340
01:13:25.053 --> 01:13:28.605
Look the trees around . . . Let’s go look in the woods."

1341
01:13:28.605 --> 01:13:35.000
The German man got out and he went and looked and he found this on the oak tree.

1342
01:13:35.000 --> 01:13:39.618
They came back and he told them, "Make a fire and have it

1343
01:13:39.618 --> 01:13:41.750
<c.p>burning hot and boil the water.

1344
01:13:41.750 --> 01:13:44.592
Have the water hot when we get back."

1345
01:13:44.592 --> 01:13:54.539
He went and he got this off of the tree and he gave it to them and they call it . . . Grandmother say he just call it [Polly- Pull?].

1346
01:13:54.539 --> 01:13:58.092
And this is a green thing; looks something like fern.

1347
01:13:58.092 --> 01:14:02.000
You would take . . . He gave him nine swallows of it.

1348
01:14:02.000 --> 01:14:04.060
<v CRAMER>It looked like the resurrection fern?

1349
01:14:04.060 --> 01:14:07.493
The one that goes . . . It pops up when it rains.

1350
01:14:07.493 --> 01:14:08.866
<v GORDWIN>I don't know honey.

1351
01:14:08.866 --> 01:14:09.896
I don't know.

1352
01:14:09.896 --> 01:14:11.955
It looked dead like you saying,

1353
01:14:11.955 --> 01:14:17.791
but when it . . . put it on there . . . When you put it in the water it come alive.

1354
01:14:17.791 --> 01:14:18.134
<v CRAMER>[Agrees].

1355
01:14:18.134 --> 01:14:19.851
<v GORDWIN>She say they soaked it.

1356
01:14:19.851 --> 01:14:25.000
Told them to soak it in that and gave him nine swallows of it.

1357
01:14:25.000 --> 01:14:25.955
<v CRAMER>And it worked?

1358
01:14:25.955 --> 01:14:26.591
<v GORDWIN>It worked.

1359
01:14:26.591 --> 01:14:32.318
And women . . . You could not take it when you're pregnant because it would make you have a miscarriage.

1360
01:14:32.318 --> 01:14:39.000
So that was always anything . . . You had a bad sore or anything your grandmother would do it with that Polly-Pull[?].

1361
01:14:39.000 --> 01:14:43.875
<v CRAMER>So it was a tree that you would put the hair in and then like as tall as

1362
01:14:43.875 --> 01:14:46.042
<c.p>the child was . . . Was that for any ailment?

1363
01:14:46.042 --> 01:14:47.667
Or was that just for asthma?

1364
01:14:47.667 --> 01:14:50.646
<v GORDWIN>No, that was just for the asthma that I know about.

1365
01:14:50.646 --> 01:14:50.917
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1366
01:14:50.917 --> 01:14:52.000
<v GORDWIN>For the asthma.

1367
01:14:52.000 --> 01:14:53.153
<v CRAMER>What about teas?

1368
01:14:53.153 --> 01:14:55.459
What about any like sarsaparilla teas?

1369
01:14:55.459 --> 01:14:57.381
<v GORDWIN>Yeah we had mamou tea.

1370
01:14:57.381 --> 01:14:59.302
Tree's right back there now.

1371
01:14:59.302 --> 01:15:04.299
We got sassafras tea that we have all those trees around here now.

1372
01:15:04.299 --> 01:15:05.452
Sassafras and mamou.

1373
01:15:05.452 --> 01:15:05.836
<v CRAMER>[Agrees].

1374
01:15:05.836 --> 01:15:10.064
<v GORDWIN>And the sassafras we would get the . . . It make the filé.

1375
01:15:10.064 --> 01:15:11.986
The leaves make the filé.

1376
01:15:11.986 --> 01:15:13.523
The root is pink.

1377
01:15:13.523 --> 01:15:14.676
A pink root.

1378
01:15:14.676 --> 01:15:21.210
The white root on the sassafras is not good to drink, but it’s . . . The red one is.

1379
01:15:21.210 --> 01:15:23.516
So we used the red one.

1380
01:15:23.516 --> 01:15:25.822
The mamou tea is so bitter.

1381
01:15:25.822 --> 01:15:32.740
It was a man that lived and he used to ride through all the time on his bicycle.

1382
01:15:32.740 --> 01:15:34.278
He sold a tea.

1383
01:15:34.278 --> 01:15:39.658
All kind of teas and roots and stuff that was good for different illness.

1384
01:15:39.658 --> 01:15:46.192
When you'd have like chicken pox and all that they would give you some of that tea.

1385
01:15:46.192 --> 01:15:50.804
Warm tea and it would run it out on you and everything.

1386
01:15:50.804 --> 01:15:56.954
Daddy used to take the tallow from a sheep, the fat, and they call it tallow.

1387
01:15:56.954 --> 01:16:02.719
I don't know, that might be a French name or something because Daddy was French.

1388
01:16:02.719 --> 01:16:05.409
They would . . . He would warm a knife

1389
01:16:05.409 --> 01:16:13.865
<c.p>and he’d take a little piece of it and put it on . . . like if you had a sore [kernel?] on your hip,

1390
01:16:13.865 --> 01:16:22.705
or in your neck, or something, and he would wipe it and make the sign of a cross on your neck with it.

1391
01:16:22.705 --> 01:16:25.779
Over the . . . and the soreness would go away.

1392
01:16:25.779 --> 01:16:26.164
<v CRAMER>Huh.

1393
01:16:26.164 --> 01:16:26.548
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees].

1394
01:16:26.548 --> 01:16:26.932
Warm.

1395
01:16:26.932 --> 01:16:28.854
A warm thing like that.

1396
01:16:28.854 --> 01:16:36.925
<v MCKEE>And Jennifer I even [ . . .?] to throw some of that away because she used to give it to me for my kids.

1397
01:16:36.925 --> 01:16:37.310
<v CRAMER>Really?

1398
01:16:37.310 --> 01:16:37.694
<v MCKEE>[Agrees]

1399
01:16:37.694 --> 01:16:38.463
<v CRAMER>It work?

1400
01:16:38.463 --> 01:16:40.000
<v MCKEE>Yeah it work!

1401
01:16:40.000 --> 01:16:43.457
<v GORDWIN>Yeah, and rub their chest with it when they have asthma.

1402
01:16:43.457 --> 01:16:44.400
<v MCKEE>And make a . . .

1403
01:16:44.400 --> 01:16:45.029
<v GORDWIN>A poultice.

1404
01:16:45.029 --> 01:16:45.657
<v CRAMER>A compress?

1405
01:16:45.657 --> 01:16:46.914
<v MCKEE>A [. . .?] you put your . . .

1406
01:16:46.914 --> 01:16:47.857
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees] On it.

1407
01:16:47.857 --> 01:16:49.743
On a little [island?] flannel cloth.

1408
01:16:49.743 --> 01:16:51.000
Put it on it.

1409
01:16:51.000 --> 01:16:52.886
Hold the warmth to your chest.

1410
01:16:52.886 --> 01:16:54.457
<v C. GORDWIN>We call it a poultice?

1411
01:16:54.457 --> 01:16:54.771
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees]

1412
01:16:54.771 --> 01:16:56.343
<v C. GORDWIN>That's what you just said?

1413
01:16:56.343 --> 01:16:59.486
<v GORDWIN>Yeah and we used the filé . . . Abscess on your teeth.

1414
01:16:59.486 --> 01:17:02.629
You make a little . . . Put a little water on it.

1415
01:17:02.629 --> 01:17:03.886
Make a little ball.

1416
01:17:03.886 --> 01:17:08.914
Put it on a piece of . . . Mother would put it on a little piece of gauze.

1417
01:17:08.914 --> 01:17:13.000
Sterile gauze and put it on your gum and hold it there.

1418
01:17:13.000 --> 01:17:14.707
<v MCKEE>Ms. Jennifer, I remember one time . . .

1419
01:17:14.707 --> 01:17:15.276
<v GORDWIN>It ruptured.

1420
01:17:15.276 --> 01:17:16.699
It would make it rupture.

1421
01:17:16.699 --> 01:17:17.837
It burst and it . . .

1422
01:17:17.837 --> 01:17:18.122
<v MCKEE>Mama.

1423
01:17:18.122 --> 01:17:19.829
<v GORDWIN>. . . would pull it to a head.

1424
01:17:19.829 --> 01:17:20.398
<v MCKEE>My brother . . .

1425
01:17:20.398 --> 01:17:25.236
<v GORDWIN>And if you have a nail stuck in your foot they would take . . . It's the salt though.

1426
01:17:25.236 --> 01:17:28.935
The fat piece of meat, it’s the salt that you cure it in.

1427
01:17:28.935 --> 01:17:32.919
And put it in your sock and sit and . . . put it in the sock.

1428
01:17:32.919 --> 01:17:39.179
Most of the time put it on you and tie it up and put a sock on it to keep it clean.

1429
01:17:39.179 --> 01:17:46.293
You hold that on your foot and it kept that open where it didn't close up and all the poison came out from the nail.

1430
01:17:46.293 --> 01:17:48.000
From that nail, you see.

1431
01:17:48.000 --> 01:17:54.701
<v MCKEE>And I remember one time when my brother was . . . my middle . . . my brother that’s . . . I'm older than him.

1432
01:17:54.701 --> 01:17:55.445
Well he . . .

1433
01:17:55.445 --> 01:17:56.190
<v GORDWIN>After her.

1434
01:17:56.190 --> 01:17:56.562
<v MCKEE>Yeah.

1435
01:17:56.562 --> 01:17:57.679
He was sick.

1436
01:17:57.679 --> 01:18:05.496
We had had some . . . Daddy had bought us some little baby chickens and then we had them up in a coop.

1437
01:18:05.496 --> 01:18:12.942
We would play under the coop and Mama said she think that's how he contracted salmonella and . . . From the chicken.

1438
01:18:12.942 --> 01:18:14.431
The odor and stuff.

1439
01:18:14.431 --> 01:18:16.292
He was real, real sick.

1440
01:18:16.292 --> 01:18:18.153
I mean really, really sick.

1441
01:18:18.153 --> 01:18:26.343
That was during the time right before . . . The children clinic I think Mama said was just getting started, Doctor Uncle was there.

1442
01:18:26.343 --> 01:18:30.810
But one of her . . . our neighbors that lived right here next door . . .

1443
01:18:30.810 --> 01:18:32.299
<v GORDWIN>He had the fever.

1444
01:18:32.299 --> 01:18:36.766
<v MCKEE>He had the fever so bad, but her mother, our neighbor’s mother . . .

1445
01:18:36.766 --> 01:18:39.000
<v GORDWIN>Was an old French woman.

1446
01:18:39.000 --> 01:18:42.500
<v MCKEE>Yeah and she made something and I remember them sticking it under the bed.

1447
01:18:42.500 --> 01:18:45.250
<v GORDWIN>She went and got the leaves that grow by a river.

1448
01:18:45.250 --> 01:18:45.500
<v MCKEE>Yeah.

1449
01:18:45.500 --> 01:18:46.750
<v GORDWIN>We have some there now.

1450
01:18:46.750 --> 01:18:47.750
<v MCKEE>Like a swamp river.

1451
01:18:47.750 --> 01:18:48.000
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

1452
01:18:48.000 --> 01:18:51.250
<v MCKEE>She came and she brought it and she stuck it under the bed.

1453
01:18:51.250 --> 01:18:53.250
<v GORDWIN>Yeah she broke a lot of the branches.

1454
01:18:53.250 --> 01:18:54.750
<v MCKEE>Because his fever was so high.

1455
01:18:54.750 --> 01:19:01.000
<v GORDWIN>And put it . . . And she say that cooled the bed off and it made it cool and it would cool him down for his fever.

1456
01:19:01.000 --> 01:19:04.000
<v MCKEE>And his fever the next day the fever just was gone.

1457
01:19:04.000 --> 01:19:04.837
<v GORDWIN>It went away.

1458
01:19:04.837 --> 01:19:08.744
The doctor was giving him all kind of medicine for fever and it stayed.

1459
01:19:08.744 --> 01:19:09.860
<v CRAMER>What was her name?

1460
01:19:09.860 --> 01:19:10.698
Do you remember?

1461
01:19:10.698 --> 01:19:11.256
<v GORDWIN>Ms. Julia.

1462
01:19:11.256 --> 01:19:12.930
All I know it’s Ms. Julia . . .

1463
01:19:12.930 --> 01:19:13.767
<v CRAMER>Was she a . . .

1464
01:19:13.767 --> 01:19:14.047
<v GORDWIN>[Mathia?]

1465
01:19:14.047 --> 01:19:14.605
<v CRAMER>Oh Mathia?

1466
01:19:14.605 --> 01:19:14.884
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

1467
01:19:14.884 --> 01:19:15.442
Frenchman, yes.

1468
01:19:15.442 --> 01:19:16.000
Mathia.

1469
01:19:16.000 --> 01:19:17.707
<v CRAMER>And was she a traiteur [faith healer]?

1470
01:19:17.707 --> 01:19:19.415
I mean did people refer to her

1471
01:19:19.415 --> 01:19:19.902
<c.p>as that?

1472
01:19:19.902 --> 01:19:21.122
<v GORDWIN>No, no. Just the neighbor.

1473
01:19:21.122 --> 01:19:21.366
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1474
01:19:21.366 --> 01:19:24.293
<v GORDWIN>Just learning things from what her parents used to do I guess.

1475
01:19:24.293 --> 01:19:26.000
<v C. GORDWIN>She was from St. Landry Parish.

1476
01:19:26.000 --> 01:19:27.500
<v GORDWIN>Yes, from St. Landry Parish.

1477
01:19:27.500 --> 01:19:29.000
<v MCKEE>From Arnaudville down that way.

1478
01:19:29.000 --> 01:19:32.900
<v GORDWIN>Yeah all of my old people like a lot of them for years . . .

1479
01:19:32.900 --> 01:19:33.200
<v MCKEE>Friends.

1480
01:19:33.200 --> 01:19:33.800
Our neighbors.

1481
01:19:33.800 --> 01:19:34.700
<v GORDWIN>Was from there.

1482
01:19:34.700 --> 01:19:38.900
They all gathered around us because Daddy was the only . . . Back in that day,

1483
01:19:38.900 --> 01:19:42.800
back in the ‘40s, Daddy was the only French person in the community.

1484
01:19:42.800 --> 01:19:43.100
Daddy . . .

1485
01:19:43.100 --> 01:19:45.200
<v MCKEE>And everybody would come to him for . . .

1486
01:19:45.200 --> 01:19:50.000
<v GORDWIN>And when the French people moved here they would come to Mother and to Daddy.

1487
01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:55.510
<v MCKEE>He would . . . Had met them at the church because he was Catholic and he would go to Saint John Bosco.

1488
01:19:55.510 --> 01:19:58.541
That's how they got to know him because they were Catholic.

1489
01:19:58.541 --> 01:20:00.194
<v GORDWIN>But we was all raised Baptist.

1490
01:20:00.194 --> 01:20:03.500
Daddy would take us to church on Sundays to the Baptist church.

1491
01:20:03.500 --> 01:20:05.429
<v CRAMER>And he went with you to both?

1492
01:20:05.429 --> 01:20:05.704
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

1493
01:20:05.704 --> 01:20:07.633
<v CRAMER>He went with you to that church?

1494
01:20:07.633 --> 01:20:09.286
<v GORDWIN>We went to both churches, yes.

1495
01:20:09.286 --> 01:20:10.112
<v MCKEE>We all did.

1496
01:20:10.112 --> 01:20:11.214
<v CRAMER>So you're like bi-religious.

1497
01:20:11.214 --> 01:20:12.041
<v GORDWIN>Catholic and that.

1498
01:20:12.041 --> 01:20:13.418
I know all the prayers.

1499
01:20:13.418 --> 01:20:17.000
<v MCKEE>When I was going to church with him it was in Latin.

1500
01:20:17.000 --> 01:20:17.623
<v CRAMER>Oh yeah?

1501
01:20:17.623 --> 01:20:18.557
<v MCKEE>Yeah, back then.

1502
01:20:18.557 --> 01:20:23.849
<v GORDWIN>First Latin. [Agrees] I used to go to church with my grandfather in New Iberia every morning.

1503
01:20:23.849 --> 01:20:26.962
We walked a mile and something to church every morning.

1504
01:20:26.962 --> 01:20:31.632
You couldn't do nothing but brush your teeth and you better not swallow no water . . .

1505
01:20:31.632 --> 01:20:31.943
<v MCKEE>Yes.

1506
01:20:31.943 --> 01:20:33.500
<v GORDWIN>Before you take your communion.

1507
01:20:33.500 --> 01:20:36.925
And I don't care if you were Sanctified, Holiness, or what.

1508
01:20:36.925 --> 01:20:40.972
You went to church with Grandpa at his church when you go there.

1509
01:20:40.972 --> 01:20:44.396
<v MCKEE>I remember one Easter and it's always stuck in my mind.

1510
01:20:44.396 --> 01:20:45.330
Easter was early.

1511
01:20:45.330 --> 01:20:50.934
Now I know it was early like early March or something, it was maybe the middle of March.

1512
01:20:50.934 --> 01:20:53.736
Mama had bought us these cute little Easter dresses.

1513
01:20:53.736 --> 01:20:59.028
When we got ready my grandfather pass we had to go to mass with him that morning.

1514
01:20:59.028 --> 01:21:03.075
It was so cold because our little Easter dresses was . . . It was spring.

1515
01:21:03.075 --> 01:21:05.566
It was spring [. . .?] and it was so cold.

1516
01:21:05.566 --> 01:21:08.057
And always remember going to mass with him.

1517
01:21:08.057 --> 01:21:09.613
We always went to mass.

1518
01:21:09.613 --> 01:21:11.792
Then we'd have to go to church.

1519
01:21:11.792 --> 01:21:15.528
We thought we was going to get out of going to church,

1520
01:21:15.528 --> 01:21:20.821
but we had to still go to church with my grandmother at the Mount Zion Baptist church.

1521
01:21:20.821 --> 01:21:23.000
<v CRAMER>You spent all day in church?

1522
01:21:23.000 --> 01:21:24.125
<v MCKEE>All day in church.

1523
01:21:24.125 --> 01:21:25.812
We should be really good people.

1524
01:21:25.812 --> 01:21:29.469
<v GORDWIN>And if you didn't go to church you couldn't go play that afternoon.

1525
01:21:29.469 --> 01:21:29.750
<v MCKEE>No.

1526
01:21:29.750 --> 01:21:30.594
<v CRAMER>There you go.

1527
01:21:30.594 --> 01:21:31.438
<v GORDWIN>I punished myself.

1528
01:21:31.438 --> 01:21:32.844
I'd stay home with them.

1529
01:21:32.844 --> 01:21:35.375
<v CRAMER>That's a good . . . Actually leads me to another question.

1530
01:21:35.375 --> 01:21:39.312
When you were growing up, what kind of discipline did your parents sort of . . .?

1531
01:21:39.312 --> 01:21:41.000
<v MCKEE>[laughs] She got beat down.

1532
01:21:41.000 --> 01:21:43.330
<v GORDWIN>My mama would beat you to death, girl.

1533
01:21:43.330 --> 01:21:46.825
They’d put her in jail . . . She'd be in the penitentiary right now.

1534
01:21:46.825 --> 01:21:48.573
But you know what I believe?

1535
01:21:48.573 --> 01:21:53.524
If they do the children that now they wouldn't have them shooting the police and everybody else.

1536
01:21:53.524 --> 01:21:57.602
And fussing with the school teacher at school and fighting them and all that.

1537
01:21:57.602 --> 01:22:00.806
Let me tell you, my mama had a little oak chair.

1538
01:22:00.806 --> 01:22:02.262
Now this is no lie.

1539
01:22:02.262 --> 01:22:05.175
Me, I’m the . . . I was the unruliest thing she had.

1540
01:22:05.175 --> 01:22:06.340
I would lay down.

1541
01:22:06.340 --> 01:22:12.165
She would make you lay down on that floor and she sat that little chair on my head across my

1542
01:22:12.165 --> 01:22:13.039
<c.p>neck like that.

1543
01:22:13.039 --> 01:22:14.495
You couldn't raise it up.

1544
01:22:14.495 --> 01:22:15.660
You couldn't get up.

1545
01:22:15.660 --> 01:22:19.738
She'd sit in that chair and she'd . . . The rest of you was for her.

1546
01:22:19.738 --> 01:22:21.194
That back side was hers.

1547
01:22:21.194 --> 01:22:26.437
<v MCKEE>Tell her how your grandmother whooped y'all one day right there on the porch on Old Spanish Trail.

1548
01:22:26.437 --> 01:22:30.806
<v GORDWIN>Yeah she reached up in the tree and got a . . . It was a gum tree.

1549
01:22:30.806 --> 01:22:33.427
<v C. GORDWIN>Tell her what y’all were doing . . . What had happened?

1550
01:22:33.427 --> 01:22:36.340
<v GORDWIN>We went to the store right here across the track.

1551
01:22:36.340 --> 01:22:39.835
We was coming back and we got right to where my house . . .

1552
01:22:39.835 --> 01:22:41.000
<v C. GORDWIN>You and who?

1553
01:22:41.000 --> 01:22:42.722
<v GORDWIN>Me and my cousin [Caswell?].

1554
01:22:42.722 --> 01:22:43.410
First cousin.

1555
01:22:43.410 --> 01:22:46.854
We walked across and we sitting on the porch, too.

1556
01:22:46.854 --> 01:22:51.675
One of us going there getting us a bucket or a basket or something.

1557
01:22:51.675 --> 01:22:57.528
We depended on this old paper bag the man put the stuff in at the store, the [ . . .?].

1558
01:22:57.528 --> 01:22:59.250
Way up in the ’40s.

1559
01:22:59.250 --> 01:23:00.627
Late ’40s, early ’50s.

1560
01:23:00.627 --> 01:23:04.071
He sit up over there hollering, "Somebody come meet us.

1561
01:23:04.071 --> 01:23:05.104
Bring us something."

1562
01:23:05.104 --> 01:23:18.877
Grandma was coming and when we saw her reach up in that tree at the corner of her yard . . . he was trying to pick them up and get where we could . . . She whipped us every step of the ways home.

1563
01:23:18.877 --> 01:23:23.698
Then she whipped us again after we got in the house with her switch.

1564
01:23:23.698 --> 01:23:25.420
It was a gum tree.

1565
01:23:25.420 --> 01:23:29.896
Then Uncle Johnny was . . . Had stuck his head in the window, "Mama quit

1566
01:23:29.896 --> 01:23:31.618
<c.p>beating them kids to death."

1567
01:23:31.618 --> 01:23:40.226
She caught him by his head and rapped him across the back and he was a grown man. [laughs] Grandma . . . My mama was, oh Lord.

1568
01:23:40.226 --> 01:23:41.948
My mama could hit you.

1569
01:23:41.948 --> 01:23:43.670
<v MCKEE>Her mama and her grandmother.

1570
01:23:43.670 --> 01:23:46.425
<v GORDWIN>Honey, could whip you with a good courage.

1571
01:23:46.425 --> 01:23:52.967
My mama would be. . . And my sister that's older than me she was always like timid, and like innocent.

1572
01:23:52.967 --> 01:23:54.000
<v C. GORDWIN>Which one?

1573
01:23:54.000 --> 01:23:54.314
<v GORDWIN>Della.

1574
01:23:54.314 --> 01:23:56.197
And then Della she's still living.

1575
01:23:56.197 --> 01:23:58.081
All of them dead but Della.

1576
01:23:58.081 --> 01:24:06.556
Aunt Della always was . . . and Mother would . . . she would have to take care of me because I was five years . . . She was five years older than me.

1577
01:24:06.556 --> 01:24:09.381
One day I'm looking at the water I guess.

1578
01:24:09.381 --> 01:24:10.637
I can't remember it,

1579
01:24:10.637 --> 01:24:18.798
but I'm looking she say like that one the edge of the porch on the back and I fell off in the little puddle of water.

1580
01:24:18.798 --> 01:24:23.821
Mother . . . She say Mother whipped her that day and told her to take care of me.

1581
01:24:23.821 --> 01:24:28.215
"I told you to take care of my baby and don't let her fall."

1582
01:24:28.215 --> 01:24:31.668
She said ever since then she’ll be taking care of me.

1583
01:24:31.668 --> 01:24:35.749
She'll come down here to see how I'm doing and all like that.

1584
01:24:35.749 --> 01:24:37.318
She still do it, so.

1585
01:24:37.318 --> 01:24:39.516
I was always doing Della Bell something.

1586
01:24:39.516 --> 01:24:42.655
If she didn't do what I said I'd fight her.

1587
01:24:42.655 --> 01:24:47.363
Honey, Mother would whip me and then Della Bell would doctor my whelps on me.

1588
01:24:47.363 --> 01:24:49.247
The little whelps the switch made.

1589
01:24:49.247 --> 01:24:51.758
She'd rub them and put something on them.

1590
01:24:51.758 --> 01:24:54.269
I think about that now since I'm grown.

1591
01:24:54.269 --> 01:25:00.547
I say, "I would have put something on it that would burn me if I’d had been . . ." [laughs] I say,

1592
01:25:00.547 --> 01:25:04.000
"I would used to be giving her a bad time."

1593
01:25:04.000 --> 01:25:08.538
And when we got all those boys . . . See, we didn't have any boys.

1594
01:25:08.538 --> 01:25:09.236
After those

1595
01:25:09.236 --> 01:25:10.981
<c.p>boys came, Lord have mercy.

1596
01:25:10.981 --> 01:25:13.425
I got meaner than a junkyard dog.

1597
01:25:13.425 --> 01:25:15.868
But I know now what it was.

1598
01:25:15.868 --> 01:25:20.755
I was jealous of the kids because everybody’s giving all their attention to them.

1599
01:25:20.755 --> 01:25:22.849
I got focused . . . All the attention.

1600
01:25:22.849 --> 01:25:26.340
And then I didn't realize, but I loved the kids.

1601
01:25:26.340 --> 01:25:30.528
I wasn't mean to them, but I was mean to the adults.

1602
01:25:30.528 --> 01:25:34.717
Oh Lord, I wouldn't agree on nothing they would say or do.

1603
01:25:34.717 --> 01:25:37.858
Just one of my sisters could comb my hair.

1604
01:25:37.858 --> 01:25:38.906
My second one.

1605
01:25:38.906 --> 01:25:41.698
She would comb my hair all the time.

1606
01:25:41.698 --> 01:25:43.094
Her name was Elaine.

1607
01:25:43.094 --> 01:25:44.491
That's the second sister.

1608
01:25:44.491 --> 01:25:50.075
I used to take care of her boys when I’d go to her house and babysit,

1609
01:25:50.075 --> 01:25:53.217
and I’d be . . . I had to clean up, too.

1610
01:25:53.217 --> 01:25:54.962
Mopping the floors and stuff.

1611
01:25:54.962 --> 01:25:58.104
I'd sit them on the couch and blindfold them.

1612
01:25:58.104 --> 01:26:02.642
One of them wouldn't keep the blindfold on, but the other one did.

1613
01:26:02.642 --> 01:26:07.528
The oldest one would sit there with the blindfold on until I would finish.

1614
01:26:07.528 --> 01:26:08.575
I was bad.

1615
01:26:08.575 --> 01:26:10.321
I was a bad kid.

1616
01:26:10.321 --> 01:26:11.368
Spoiled like rotten.

1617
01:26:11.368 --> 01:26:15.906
Just did anything I thought I wanted to do I would do it.

1618
01:26:15.906 --> 01:26:19.047
Always Mother say, "Never dare her to do anything."

1619
01:26:19.047 --> 01:26:23.585
Because I always did what I wanted to do since I was little.

1620
01:26:23.585 --> 01:26:29.519
That's why she had to whip me so because she say I did not listen to anything.

1621
01:26:29.519 --> 01:26:35.104
Say, "Just let her do what she want to do because she's going to do it.

1622
01:26:35.104 --> 01:26:39.991
She'll just have to learn, if it's wrong, she'll just have to learn it.”

1623
01:26:39.991 --> 01:26:42.783
But Mother would try to change my mind.

1624
01:26:42.783 --> 01:26:43.830
But she didn't.

1625
01:26:43.830 --> 01:26:47.670
I’d just do what I want to do all the time.

1626
01:26:47.670 --> 01:26:51.858
And say what I wanted to say and I'm still like that.

1627
01:26:51.858 --> 01:26:53.604
And I'm looking at this . . .

1628
01:26:53.604 --> 01:26:55.000
<v CRAMER>The newspaper clipping.

1629
01:26:55.000 --> 01:26:56.083
<v GORDWIN>He won mayor.

1630
01:26:56.083 --> 01:26:56.805
<v CRAMER>Gerald Washington.

1631
01:26:56.805 --> 01:26:58.609
<v MCKEE>But that was in Westlake.

1632
01:26:58.609 --> 01:27:01.496
<v GORDWIN>He went to school right here in Mossville.

1633
01:27:01.496 --> 01:27:02.579
He won mayor.

1634
01:27:02.579 --> 01:27:11.962
And before it was time for him to take office, they had done swore him in, and everybody went off to party for the New Year’s.

1635
01:27:11.962 --> 01:27:15.211
He stayed in and he had the books examined.

1636
01:27:15.211 --> 01:27:18.098
The books was not good and he died.

1637
01:27:18.098 --> 01:27:22.068
They say he killed himself, but I say they killed him.

1638
01:27:22.068 --> 01:27:23.511
I'll still say it.

1639
01:27:23.511 --> 01:27:26.038
<v MCKEE>But he was the mayor of Westlake.

1640
01:27:26.038 --> 01:27:32.534
<v GORDWIN>And if somebody . . . If anybody kill me you can say it was the truth because it was true.

1641
01:27:32.534 --> 01:27:33.617
They killed him.

1642
01:27:33.617 --> 01:27:36.504
<v MCKEE>But that was . . . He was down in Westlake.

1643
01:27:36.504 --> 01:27:39.030
<v GORDWIN>And you know what they doing now?

1644
01:27:39.030 --> 01:27:43.000
They got a fight about Westlake ain’t got no money.

1645
01:27:43.000 --> 01:27:44.156
<v CRAMER>Well you know what,

1646
01:27:44.156 --> 01:27:51.378
I mean one day we were talking and I'd love to get this on record too just for the heck of it for the stories . . .

1647
01:27:51.378 --> 01:27:53.111
<v C. GORDWIN>His body was found in Mossville.

1648
