1769
01:31:01.292 --> 01:31:03.020
<v GORDWIN>My mom always had like . . .

1770
01:31:03.020 --> 01:31:04.748
<v MCKEE>I remember the house though.

1771
01:31:04.748 --> 01:31:07.166
<v GORDWIN>Do ice cream and tea and stuff.

1772
01:31:07.166 --> 01:31:10.276
We used to drink mint tea on Sunday afternoons.

1773
01:31:10.276 --> 01:31:13.040
She had tea planted right in her yard.

1774
01:31:13.040 --> 01:31:16.495
We picked the tea, and make tea, and she would

1775
01:31:16.495 --> 01:31:19.605
<c.p>serve the tea, and she'd make homemade ice cream.

1776
01:31:19.605 --> 01:31:24.442
I remember one day one of those . . . Mr. Edwards’ brothers we call him [Steena?].

1777
01:31:24.442 --> 01:31:25.824
You didn't know him.

1778
01:31:25.824 --> 01:31:27.206
<v MCKEE>I heard the name.

1779
01:31:27.206 --> 01:31:29.625
<v GORDWIN>Name talk . . . Nannie and them talk about.

1780
01:31:29.625 --> 01:31:36.189
He say, "Ms. Lemelle, you going to give this good ice cream in a little old bowl like this?"

1781
01:31:36.189 --> 01:31:38.953
And Mother just wanted . . . He was always funny.

1782
01:31:38.953 --> 01:31:42.409
Mother say, "Alright Steena I'll give you a bigger bowl."

1783
01:31:42.409 --> 01:31:48.973
Got a big old crock bowl which you make cakes in and put him some ice cream in that.

1784
01:31:48.973 --> 01:31:57.266
Then he said, "Oh wait now Ms. Ida Mae you gave me the big old bowl you got to give me a big spoon."

1785
01:31:57.266 --> 01:32:00.030
And she brought him that big cook spoon.

1786
01:32:00.030 --> 01:32:03.485
That was the biggest laugh you’d ever want to see.

1787
01:32:03.485 --> 01:32:05.213
They would just have fun.

1788
01:32:05.213 --> 01:32:06.249
That’s the fun.

1789
01:32:06.249 --> 01:32:08.322
We'd play ball on Sunday afternoon.

1790
01:32:08.322 --> 01:32:09.359
Everybody get together.

1791
01:32:09.359 --> 01:32:13.159
Group walk up to Perkins Town, that's what we call it,

1792
01:32:13.159 --> 01:32:21.106
and a group would walk from Perkins Town on this end and we would have ball games, and play, and eat ice cream.

1793
01:32:21.106 --> 01:32:25.252
Then we'd go up to Saprack one day and all of this.

1794
01:32:25.252 --> 01:32:29.399
Up that way and Aunt [Ma?] would always play ball with us.

1795
01:32:29.399 --> 01:32:30.435
Miss . . . A lady.

1796
01:32:30.435 --> 01:32:31.817
Her name was Marcella.

1797
01:32:31.817 --> 01:32:36.309
She was born a LaDoux, but she was a . . . Married a Rigmaiden man.

1798
01:32:36.309 --> 01:32:37.000
<v CRAMER>Baseball?

1799
01:32:37.000 --> 01:32:38.071
<v GORDWIN>We played baseball.

1800
01:32:38.071 --> 01:32:39.143
<v CRAMER>And softball, or?

1801
01:32:39.143 --> 01:32:39.857
<v GORDWIN>Softball, baseball . . .

1802
01:32:39.857 --> 01:32:42.714
<v MCKEE>We had a little negro league here in . . .

1803
01:32:42.714 --> 01:32:43.071
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

1804
01:32:43.071 --> 01:32:44.143
<v MCKEE>In the ’50s.

1805
01:32:44.143 --> 01:32:48.429
Mama said when her brother-in-law was it when Uncle Richard came home?

1806
01:32:48.429 --> 01:32:49.500
<v GORDWIN>In the ’60s.

1807
01:32:49.500 --> 01:32:52.357
<v MCKEE>Was it in the ’60s or early ’60s?

1808
01:32:52.357 --> 01:32:52.714
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees]

1809
01:32:52.714 --> 01:32:54.857
<v CRAMER>What was the name of it?

1810
01:32:54.857 --> 01:32:57.000
Do you remember the team?

1811
01:32:57.000 --> 01:32:57.600
<v GORDWIN>I done forgot.

1812
01:32:57.600 --> 01:32:59.000
<v CRAMER>We can probably look that up to . . .

1813
01:32:59.000 --> 01:32:59.400
<v GORDWIN>Just Mossville.

1814
01:32:59.400 --> 01:33:01.600
I don't know if they had it on record or not.

1815
01:33:01.600 --> 01:33:02.200
<v CRAMER>Like the mascot?

1816
01:33:02.200 --> 01:33:03.000
<v GORDWIN>They just played.

1817
01:33:03.000 --> 01:33:04.814
<v MCKEE>Some of the older guys might . . .

1818
01:33:04.814 --> 01:33:06.023
<v GORDWIN>Got together and played.

1819
01:33:06.023 --> 01:33:06.326
<v MCKEE>Stayed.

1820
01:33:06.326 --> 01:33:09.651
Some of their children might know if they had a name.

1821
01:33:09.651 --> 01:33:11.163
<v C. GORDWIN>It was earlier than that.

1822
01:33:11.163 --> 01:33:13.279
<v MCKEE>It had to be in the ’50s.

1823
01:33:13.279 --> 01:33:14.791
Early ’50s around the ’50s.

1824
01:33:14.791 --> 01:33:17.512
Uncle Richard had done came home from the war.

1825
01:33:17.512 --> 01:33:19.023
He played before he went.

1826
01:33:19.023 --> 01:33:22.047
<v GORDWIN>[Disagrees] I didn't know him before he went to war.

1827
01:33:22.047 --> 01:33:24.163
<v MCKEE>So it was probably in the ’50s.

1828
01:33:24.163 --> 01:33:25.070
<v GORDWIN>He was married.

1829
01:33:25.070 --> 01:33:26.581
Them boys was little then.

1830
01:33:26.581 --> 01:33:26.884
<v C. GORDWIN>Yes.

1831
01:33:26.884 --> 01:33:29.907
<v MCKEE>Yes so that was in the ’50s not the ’60s.

1832
01:33:29.907 --> 01:33:34.744
<v GORDWIN>[Agrees] They had a team in the ’60s, though, down behind where the old fruits . . . [?] Daddy?

1833
01:33:34.744 --> 01:33:36.860
That club used to be a nightclub.

1834
01:33:36.860 --> 01:33:38.070
They had one then.

1835
01:33:38.070 --> 01:33:42.000
<v CRAMER>So where was your . . . Like so, where did you get groceries from?

1836
01:33:42.000 --> 01:33:46.317
<v GORDWIN>Store right across the track Garrett's, and in Westlake, and in Sulphur.

1837
01:33:46.317 --> 01:33:46.676
<v CRAMER>Okay.

1838
01:33:46.676 --> 01:33:48.835
<v GORDWIN>Garrett's carried a little community store.

1839
01:33:48.835 --> 01:33:50.633
<v CRAMER>Because you grew a lot.

1840
01:33:50.633 --> 01:33:50.993
<v GORDWIN>Yes.

1841
01:33:50.993 --> 01:33:53.871
<v CRAMER>And then you raised your own livestock, so . . .

1842
01:33:53.871 --> 01:33:55.309
<v GORDWIN>We growed everything, yeah.

1843
01:33:55.309 --> 01:34:00.705
Fruit and stuff, we had that and Daddy would bring us fruit from Lake Charles.

1844
01:34:00.705 --> 01:34:04.302
Down that way we had a store down there, too.

1845
01:34:04.302 --> 01:34:05.381
<v MCKEE>On west end.

1846
01:34:05.381 --> 01:34:07.540
<v GORDWIN>Now that's where the killing was.

1847
01:34:07.540 --> 01:34:08.619
On that end.

1848
01:34:08.619 --> 01:34:12.576
Man killed a woman right in the middle of the street.

1849
01:34:12.576 --> 01:34:14.374
Shot her with a shotgun.

1850
01:34:14.374 --> 01:34:16.892
<v C. GORDWIN>What year and who was the one?

1851
01:34:16.892 --> 01:34:24.806
<v GORDWIN>That was in the ’40s I'm sure, or the early ’50s because Maplewood . . . Mother and them was in Maplewood cleaning the houses.

1852
01:34:24.806 --> 01:34:32.000
After they built the houses they would clean all the saw dust and get them ready to move in.

1853
01:34:32.000 --> 01:34:35.048
<v MCKEE>Around the time I guess Cities Service was being built.

1854
01:34:35.048 --> 01:34:35.657
<v GORDWIN>Built. [Agrees].

1855
01:34:35.657 --> 01:34:38.095
The man was in here from upstate somewhere.

1856
01:34:38.095 --> 01:34:42.971
I don't know if he was from Chicago, or New York and he killed the lady.

1857
01:34:42.971 --> 01:34:44.190
<v C. GORDWIN>What was her name?

1858
01:34:44.190 --> 01:34:45.714
<v MCKEE>Tell them the name, Mama.

1859
01:34:45.714 --> 01:34:47.238
<v GORDWIN>Her name was [Early?] Braxton.

1860
01:34:47.238 --> 01:34:49.067
<v MCKEE>And her mama had a what?

1861
01:34:49.067 --> 01:34:50.590
What was her mother name?

1862
01:34:50.590 --> 01:34:50.895
<v GORDWIN>Martill.

1863
01:34:50.895 --> 01:34:52.419
<v MCKEE>And what did she have?

1864
01:34:52.419 --> 01:34:53.333
A little canteen?

1865
01:34:53.333 --> 01:34:53.638
<v GORDWIN>Yeah.

1866
01:34:53.638 --> 01:35:00.952
They had it on . . . That was on this side of the road and they was building a bigger place across the street from it.

1867
01:35:00.952 --> 01:35:02.171
<v C. GORDWIN>On Old Spanish Trail.

1868
01:35:02.171 --> 01:35:04.000
<v GORDWIN>On Old Spanish Trail [agrees].

1869
01:35:04.000 --> 01:35:06.171
<v CRAMER>So why did he kill her?

1870
01:35:06.171 --> 01:35:07.619
<v GORDWIN>She was a floozy.

1871
01:35:07.619 --> 01:35:08.343
<v MCKEE>Fast woman.

1872
01:35:08.343 --> 01:35:10.876
<v CRAMER>So it was a crime of passion?

1873
01:35:10.876 --> 01:35:12.686
<v GORDWIN>And I used to . . . Yes.

1874
01:35:12.686 --> 01:35:14.495
<v C. GORDWIN>Right, a crime of passion.

1875
01:35:14.495 --> 01:35:20.648
<v GORDWIN>I saw her when I was a girl, a little girl, she would pass this Bradley boy.

1876
01:35:20.648 --> 01:35:27.524
What I was telling you his mother had the little club . . . he would always work cars . . . make . . . do cars.

1877
01:35:27.524 --> 01:35:28.971
<v C. GORDWIN>Not him his . . . Who?

1878
01:35:28.971 --> 01:35:31.505
<v GORDWIN>[Zeke?] would always make cars . . . Do cars.

1879
01:35:31.505 --> 01:35:34.400
He would . . . He had a little convertible car.

1880
01:35:34.400 --> 01:35:39.829
She would pass in it and she'd have a coat with the fur on it.

1881
01:35:39.829 --> 01:35:42.000
<v C. GORDWIN>She was a beautiful woman.

1882
01:35:42.000 --> 01:35:43.757
<v GORDWIN>Yeah she was very pretty.

1883
01:35:43.757 --> 01:35:50.081
She was light skinned and she had her hair always was pressed I guess with a hot comb.

1884
01:35:50.081 --> 01:35:51.486
It would be hanging.

1885
01:35:51.486 --> 01:35:55.351
It would be blowing and she'd pass driving that little car.

1886
01:35:55.351 --> 01:36:00.270
So I say, all these old boys now I know what was going on.

1887
01:36:00.270 --> 01:36:02.027
She was flirting with everybody.

1888
01:36:02.027 --> 01:36:03.784
All the little country boys.

1889
01:36:03.784 --> 01:36:07.649
They'd get in the woods and laugh and talk about it.

1890
01:36:07.649 --> 01:36:11.162
And go gamble in the woods and talk about stuff.

1891
01:36:11.162 --> 01:36:15.027
They had a little gambling place they'd gamble in the woods.

1892
01:36:15.027 --> 01:36:21.000
And then . . . But they didn't get mad at one another and fight one another about the woman.

1893
01:36:21.000 --> 01:36:25.568
But this man came in here making money and working at the refinery.

1894
01:36:25.568 --> 01:36:29.784
She thought she could play him like that and he killed her.

1895
01:36:29.784 --> 01:36:35.757
He went to my sister's house . . . And he was friends with my sister's husband from the job.

1896
01:36:35.757 --> 01:36:45.595
He went to my sister's house that morning and he say he was just looking at the gun in the corner . . . And her brother-in-law’s always stay with her.

1897
01:36:45.595 --> 01:36:54.027
They was young adult men because they was living way back in the woods like and they always stayed there at home with her

1898
01:36:54.027 --> 01:36:56.135
<c.p>because she had a little baby.

1899
01:36:56.135 --> 01:37:04.919
That was they job to take care of their sister-in-law in the daytime when their brother was at school and they daddy was at work.

1900
01:37:04.919 --> 01:37:10.892
They was there and he just told her, "Why y'all stay here all the time like that?"

1901
01:37:10.892 --> 01:37:16.162
They said, "Because our daddy told us to stay here and take care of May."

1902
01:37:16.162 --> 01:37:19.324
They would call my sister May all the time.

1903
01:37:19.324 --> 01:37:20.730
Her name was Marion.

1904
01:37:20.730 --> 01:37:27.054
So he said . . . Oh, and Marion say he just kept a looking and looking, but then he left.

1905
01:37:27.054 --> 01:37:39.000
And he waited until this girl's mother left home and he broke in the house and stole the shotgun and the shells and came back down and killed her at her little canteen.

1906
01:37:39.000 --> 01:37:39.994
She ran out.

1907
01:37:39.994 --> 01:37:45.298
A man was there, named Mr. Johnson, Jim Johnson, he was wiring a new . . . The place.

1908
01:37:45.298 --> 01:37:54.910
He ran . . . He told him to leave because he had something to do and he didn't want him to be in it because you're not the cause of it.

1909
01:37:54.910 --> 01:37:57.893
So the man say he didn't leave right away.

1910
01:37:57.893 --> 01:38:00.876
He told him, "Well I'm leaving after a while."

1911
01:38:00.876 --> 01:38:04.522
He said but he said, "I want you to leave now."

1912
01:38:04.522 --> 01:38:09.494
The man said he just went to go across the street to get his thing.

1913
01:38:09.494 --> 01:38:17.118
Said when he looked up the girl was coming out screaming and hollering running and the man was behind her with the gun.

1914
01:38:17.118 --> 01:38:19.438
He interceded and tried to stop him.

1915
01:38:19.438 --> 01:38:25.736
He hit him in the head with the barrel, butt of the gun, and he fell in the ditch.

1916
01:38:25.736 --> 01:38:28.388
Knocked him out and fell in the ditch.

1917
01:38:28.388 --> 01:38:35.017
And he say by that time the bread truck was coming to put . . . drop bread at the place buying bread.

1918
01:38:35.017 --> 01:38:37.006
And he . . . Is he recording this?

1919
01:38:37.006 --> 01:38:37.337
<v CRAMER>[Agrees]

1920
01:38:37.337 --> 01:38:38.000
<v KYLE TANGLAO>Yes.

1921
01:38:38.000 --> 01:38:39.000
<v GORDWIN>Oh and he . . .

1922
01:38:39.000 --> 01:38:41.000
<v CRAMER>It's a historic murder, it's okay.

1923
01:38:41.000 --> 01:38:41.667
<v GORDWIN>It's alright.

1924
01:38:41.667 --> 01:38:43.000
<v MCKEE>Go ahead Mama.

1925
01:38:43.000 --> 01:38:47.590
<v GORDWIN>He fell in the ditch and he said the water come up over his face.

1926
01:38:47.590 --> 01:38:51.261
He started to get up, but he say, "I better stay here."

1927
01:38:51.261 --> 01:38:56.769
And just when the bread truck drove down, the lady ran around the truck and told to leave,

1928
01:38:56.769 --> 01:38:59.216
"Go I need . . . He trying to kill me."

1929
01:38:59.216 --> 01:39:07.478
Said the man got so scared he just pull off and left her standing in the road and he just drawed the gun on and shot her.

1930
01:39:07.478 --> 01:39:09.007
Right there in the street.

1931
01:39:09.007 --> 01:39:09.313
And . . .

1932
01:39:09.313 --> 01:39:09.925
<v MCKEE>He ran.

1933
01:39:09.925 --> 01:39:12.985
<v GORDWIN>He came back to the ditch and looked at him.

1934
01:39:12.985 --> 01:39:15.127
Saying he played like he was dead.

1935
01:39:15.127 --> 01:39:20.022
He say he kicked him and he hit him again with the gun and walked off.

1936
01:39:20.022 --> 01:39:22.164
He shot her twice in her stomach.

1937
01:39:22.164 --> 01:39:24.000
<v MCKEE>That's when they were all across . . .

1938
01:39:24.000 --> 01:39:31.343
<v GORDWIN>We were across the road from my house washing clothes and the man that got hit in the head he was on his bicycle.

1939
01:39:31.343 --> 01:39:33.791
He said, "You kids get in the house.

1940
01:39:33.791 --> 01:39:35.321
Something happened down the road."

1941
01:39:35.321 --> 01:39:37.157
Jay B. they called the man.

1942
01:39:37.157 --> 01:39:40.828
Jay B. that's all I ever knowed his name was Jay B.

1943
01:39:40.828 --> 01:39:43.582
<c.p>He came up say, "He's gone crazy or something.

1944
01:39:43.582 --> 01:39:45.418
He just killed Early down there.

1945
01:39:45.418 --> 01:39:48.172
Y'all get in the house and lock the doors.

1946
01:39:48.172 --> 01:39:50.619
Put your windows down and lock the doors."

1947
01:39:50.619 --> 01:39:51.537
We did that.

1948
01:39:51.537 --> 01:39:53.373
He said, "Y'all mama not home."

1949
01:39:53.373 --> 01:39:55.209
He said, "Get in the house."

1950
01:39:55.209 --> 01:39:57.963
And we ran in the house and did that.

1951
01:39:57.963 --> 01:40:01.022
Me, and Elaine, Pearly May, and Della Bell was home.

1952
01:40:01.022 --> 01:40:03.776
<v MCKEE>And then she said the body stayed out there.

1953
01:40:03.776 --> 01:40:05.000
She told us.

1954
01:40:05.000 --> 01:40:06.926
<v GORDWIN>All day long in the road.

1955
01:40:06.926 --> 01:40:13.346
But they picked it up off the road and brought it on the porch and put a sheet over it.

1956
01:40:13.346 --> 01:40:19.123
Then when Mother got home and the sheriff came . . . It took the sheriff so long to get there.

1957
01:40:19.123 --> 01:40:21.691
<v MCKEE>Who was the sheriff at that time, Mama?

1958
01:40:21.691 --> 01:40:24.901
<v GORDWIN>The man . . . We didn't have any phones out there then.

1959
01:40:24.901 --> 01:40:27.148
The man was old man Ham Reed.

1960
01:40:27.148 --> 01:40:27.790
<v MCKEE>Ham Reed.

1961
01:40:27.790 --> 01:40:29.074
<v GORDWIN>Ham Reed and he . . .

1962
01:40:29.074 --> 01:40:31.000
<v MCKEE>Ham Reed Senior, or Junior?

1963
01:40:31.000 --> 01:40:31.333
<v GORDWIN>Senior.

1964
01:40:31.333 --> 01:40:31.667
<v MCKEE>Senior.

1965
01:40:31.667 --> 01:40:33.000
Ham Reed Senior.

1966
01:40:33.000 --> 01:40:33.981
<v GORDWIN>And he came.

1967
01:40:33.981 --> 01:40:43.462
They came out and investigated it and then that's when they let us . . . They’d say we could go and see and we went down there and looked at it.

1968
01:40:43.462 --> 01:40:45.096
The body on the porch.

1969
01:40:45.096 --> 01:40:48.692
That always made me know never to do the wrong thing.

1970
01:40:48.692 --> 01:40:54.577
Always do the right thing because somebody's not going to like you if you do the wrong thing.

1971
01:40:54.577 --> 01:40:59.154
I learned from that day the irony of a woman being a fast woman.

1972
01:40:59.154 --> 01:41:00.135
There it went.

1973
01:41:00.135 --> 01:41:02.750
And see all of that I was young.

1974
01:41:02.750 --> 01:41:07.654
I don't know if I was even in school and I calculated all of that.

1975
01:41:07.654 --> 01:41:08.962
This is not nice.

1976
01:41:08.962 --> 01:41:12.558
You used to see that lady passing in that boy's car.

1977
01:41:12.558 --> 01:41:15.500
She used to be with this guy, that guy,

1978
01:41:15.500 --> 01:41:21.385
and I kind of knew that like from a child that you don't do these things like that.

1979
01:41:21.385 --> 01:41:23.019
<v CRAMER>So what happened to him?

1980
01:41:23.019 --> 01:41:24.000
The murderer?

1981
01:41:24.000 --> 01:41:29.284
<v GORDWIN>Oh, he went in the woods behind her grandfather's house where he was staying and killed himself.

1982
01:41:29.284 --> 01:41:31.149
The boys found him the woods.

1983
01:41:31.149 --> 01:41:37.365
They were playing in the woods and they found . . . Stan and Uncle [Brock?] and them found him in the woods.

1984
01:41:37.365 --> 01:41:42.649
<v MCKEE>They said they put him on a train I heard people saying they don't know what happened.

1985
01:41:42.649 --> 01:41:45.135
Don’t know where they took him back to.

1986
01:41:45.135 --> 01:41:47.311
They don't know if he was cremated.

1987
01:41:47.311 --> 01:41:49.797
<v GORDWIN>Probably sent him to where he came from.

1988
01:41:49.797 --> 01:41:50.730
<v CRAMER>Where he’s from.

1989
01:41:50.730 --> 01:41:51.041
Right.

1990
01:41:51.041 --> 01:41:52.905
<v MCKEE>Whatever camp he was with working.

1991
01:41:52.905 --> 01:41:54.770
<v GORDWIN>Yeah because we had a camp.

1992
01:41:54.770 --> 01:41:56.635
They had a camp down there.

1993
01:41:56.635 --> 01:41:58.189
They call it Gable Lodges.

1994
01:41:58.189 --> 01:42:01.608
Now they [ . . .?] saying about the camp over in Carlyss, huh Kim?

1995
01:42:01.608 --> 01:42:02.541
Isn’t it called?

1996
01:42:02.541 --> 01:42:02.851
<v MCKEE>Yeah.

1997
01:42:02.851 --> 01:42:05.649
And the one they built down at the docks.

1998
01:42:05.649 --> 01:42:11.554
<v GORDWIN>They had a camp right there in Maplewood for the men that came from out of town to work.

1999
01:42:11.554 --> 01:42:13.419
We used to go over there.

2000
01:42:13.419 --> 01:42:15.284
Mother and them would go clean.

2001
01:42:15.284 --> 01:42:19.635
It have a man that'd be with us because it was a man camp.

2002
01:42:19.635 --> 01:42:28.338
A man would go with us to over there and we all . . . they all . . . Mother would take me with her because she wouldn't leave me home by myself.

2003
01:42:28.338 --> 01:42:29.892
Me and Marion was there.

2004
01:42:29.892 --> 01:42:32.068
I was with Mother all the time.

2005
01:42:32.068 --> 01:42:34.554
Right by Mother and Marion all the time.

2006
01:42:34.554 --> 01:42:41.703
I was right there with them and they would cleanup those rooms and make beds, change beds, wash sheets, and stuff like that.

2007
01:42:41.703 --> 01:42:44.811
The man would be there with us all the time.

2008
01:42:44.811 --> 01:42:46.365
A man would be there.

2009
01:42:46.365 --> 01:42:48.851
Ms. Loreen was with us and Aunt Etta.

2010
01:42:48.851 --> 01:42:50.716
We all . . . They all worked together.

2011
01:42:50.716 --> 01:42:53.203
We always went there and cleaned and everything.

2012
01:42:53.203 --> 01:42:56.000
Mother would always . . . They were cleaning making money.

2013
01:42:56.000 --> 01:43:01.124
<v CRAMER>So was it camp for the men who were out of town working at . . .?

2014
01:43:01.124 --> 01:43:01.856
<v GORDWIN>Cities Service.

2015
01:43:01.856 --> 01:43:02.588
<v CRAMER>Cities Services.

2016
01:43:02.588 --> 01:43:06.248
Now previously, like before the industry came in, were there

2017
01:43:06.248 --> 01:43:08.078
<c.p>like similar camps for lumber?

2018
01:43:08.078 --> 01:43:11.007
People that came in from out of town?

2019
01:43:11.007 --> 01:43:12.837
<v GORDWIN>Not that I know about.

2020
01:43:12.837 --> 01:43:13.935
They had lumberjacks.

2021
01:43:13.935 --> 01:43:19.791
Grandfather say he cut wood over on the trees . . . Cypress on lake there in Lake Charles.

2022
01:43:19.791 --> 01:43:23.085
The old way that you went into Lake Charles.

2023
01:43:23.085 --> 01:43:31.869
The old bridge through Westlake he cut . . . he say . . . Because Grandfather was short and he say the water would be up over his chest.

2024
01:43:31.869 --> 01:43:32.967
He saw it.

2025
01:43:32.967 --> 01:43:38.092
He say but men taller than him it would be like to they waist.

2026
01:43:38.092 --> 01:43:39.556
Up to their waist.

2027
01:43:39.556 --> 01:43:41.020
Grandfather was real short.

2028
01:43:41.020 --> 01:43:44.680
That's why we always said, "Somebody Chinese in this family."

2029
01:43:44.680 --> 01:43:48.706
And that's what we found out Grandfather's mother was a Chinaman.

2030
01:43:48.706 --> 01:43:50.536
His grandmother I should say.

2031
01:43:50.536 --> 01:43:52.000
His grandmother was.

2032
01:43:52.000 --> 01:43:56.663
<v CRAMER>So I have one question for you to close it out.

2033
01:43:56.663 --> 01:44:15.315
Then we'll pick up next time we come back with maybe some more stuff about some of the places in the community where you were growing up as we kind of got into some of those like Perkins Town and that sort of thing.

2034
01:44:15.315 --> 01:44:25.065
We'll talk about the ’50s and some of the Voting Rights Act and how industry . . . When first industry that came kind of changed.

2035
01:44:25.065 --> 01:44:28.457
And we sort of started touching on that.

2036
01:44:28.457 --> 01:44:35.239
My question for you to close out this interview is what does community mean to you?

2037
01:44:35.239 --> 01:44:39.054
<v GORDWIN>Community means to me is togetherness, love, and prosperity.

2038
01:44:39.054 --> 01:44:43.293
And that, I don't know if I'll ever get again.

2039
01:44:43.293 --> 01:44:53.043
That's why I say they could never pay me enough money to cover that in my heart, in my mind, and my soul.

2040
01:44:53.043 --> 01:45:00.674
But I think Sasol is paying a fair deal for the property and the house to be moved.

2041
01:45:00.674 --> 01:45:09.152
It's . . . But I still say what should be did before I got to . . . I talked with mister . . . all the time.

2042
01:45:09.152 --> 01:45:11.696
The man that . . . John, not John.

2043
01:45:11.696 --> 01:45:14.239
The man that did the program.

2044
01:45:14.239 --> 01:45:15.511
What's his name?

2045
01:45:15.511 --> 01:45:16.359
Mr. Mitchell.

2046
01:45:16.359 --> 01:45:21.870
John Mitchell and tell him he should get in the community and see

2047
01:45:21.870 --> 01:45:27.804
<c.p>white or black, pink or navy blue people and see what the community feel.

2048
01:45:27.804 --> 01:45:30.772
Then you draw your plan by that.

2049
01:45:30.772 --> 01:45:42.641
Because, now he didn't know that I had to share . . . I never . . . my lawyer told me . . . Well which the lady's a lawyer that Daddy worked for was millionaire.

2050
01:45:42.641 --> 01:45:47.728
She took care of me and my children like with law stuff.

2051
01:45:47.728 --> 01:45:54.087
Anything I had to go to a law for to . . . She took care of it.

2052
01:45:54.087 --> 01:45:56.207
We went to her lawyer.

2053
01:45:56.207 --> 01:45:56.630
Alright.

2054
01:45:56.630 --> 01:46:00.022
They always did what was good for us.

2055
01:46:00.022 --> 01:46:09.772
Now I always say he told me to always leave my property with the inheritance of . . . Listen it's making a point to John.

2056
01:46:09.772 --> 01:46:14.859
With the inheritance that I have . . . The older children inherited Daddy's share.

2057
01:46:14.859 --> 01:46:25.457
He said leave it like that because if you go down a road and have a wreck they can sue you for your property fast.

2058
01:46:25.457 --> 01:46:28.000
It was a new law.

2059
01:46:28.000 --> 01:46:36.640
Alright now, he didn't know that I . . . when I do this if my children don't make exceptionally good to me and give me their share,

2060
01:46:36.640 --> 01:46:41.824
I still won't have enough to go and buy me a decent place to live.

2061
01:46:41.824 --> 01:46:42.860
I'm comfortable here.

2062
01:46:42.860 --> 01:46:47.699
I'm not living like a millionaire, or a half millionaire, but I'm very comfortable.

2063
01:46:47.699 --> 01:46:51.154
I've been here fifty-eight years or more and I'm comfortable.

2064
01:46:51.154 --> 01:46:57.375
Now when I'm going to have to go see about finding another place to live and to live,

2065
01:46:57.375 --> 01:47:03.250
I don't know if I'm going to be as comfortable here as . . . There as I am here.

2066
01:47:03.250 --> 01:47:09.816
And it might be even better than here, but I don't know if it'll be as comfortable for me.

2067
01:47:09.816 --> 01:47:12.926
That's all I got to say about moving, leaving.

2068
01:47:12.926 --> 01:47:15.000
<v C. GORDWIN>She didn't ask you that.

2069
01:47:15.000 --> 01:47:18.922
<v GORDWIN>No, I know what she asked me but I'm saying that.

2070
01:47:18.922 --> 01:47:23.202
And y’all tend to y'all business and I'm tending to my business.

2071
01:47:23.202 --> 01:47:24.984
Y'all still young; I'm old.

2072
01:47:24.984 --> 01:47:29.977
<v CRAMER>So let me ask you this then just to follow up on that question.

2073
01:47:29.977 --> 01:47:32.116
What does home mean to you?

2074
01:47:32.116 --> 01:47:33.899
<v GORDWIN>Home means peace and tranquility.

2075
01:47:33.899 --> 01:47:36.039
That's what it means to me.

2076
01:47:36.039 --> 01:47:37.109
Peace and tranquility.

2077
01:47:37.109 --> 01:47:47.093
It should be a place where everybody can go that's born into that family anytime that they would like to and have a place . . . Always feel safe there.

2078
01:47:47.093 --> 01:47:50.659
I have a first cousin that comes to see me.

2079
01:47:50.659 --> 01:47:51.729
His mother's dead.

2080
01:47:51.729 --> 01:47:56.364
He came here right before Christmas and he said they were doing shrimp.

2081
01:47:56.364 --> 01:48:00.643
They had shrimps and they was frying, and cleaning, and doing this.

2082
01:48:00.643 --> 01:48:10.271
And he say, "Aunt Judy I just had to come and be with y'all for a day or so because I feel safe here at your house."

2083
01:48:10.271 --> 01:48:13.481
That's what everybody tell me when they come here.

2084
01:48:13.481 --> 01:48:16.333
They feel so comfortable and relaxed and safe.

2085
01:48:16.333 --> 01:48:17.760
Charles daughter comes here.

2086
01:48:17.760 --> 01:48:20.612
She . . . Her grandmother have a very nice place.

2087
01:48:20.612 --> 01:48:24.891
Have big nice house with all kind of nice furniture in it.

2088
01:48:24.891 --> 01:48:29.884
Her mother's mama when she come here you know where she like to lay?

2089
01:48:29.884 --> 01:48:37.729
Under the table in there in the kitchen and I have a heater that I keep warm . . . My feet and knees warm.

2090
01:48:37.729 --> 01:48:47.000
She'll pull that heater out where it is and she'll get her pillow and a sleeping bag and get under the table and go sleep.

2091
01:48:47.000 --> 01:48:47.806
<v CH. GORDWIN>She's eighteen.

2092
01:48:47.806 --> 01:48:52.236
<v GORDWIN>She's eighteen years old and she's tall and skinny like you.

2093
01:48:52.236 --> 01:48:59.083
<v CRAMER>Well I must say I feel very comfortable here and I really appreciate you . . . Oh she's beautiful.

2094
01:48:59.083 --> 01:49:00.292
They're all beautiful.

2095
01:49:00.292 --> 01:49:01.903
I like the bowtie.

2096
01:49:01.903 --> 01:49:03.514
<v GORDWIN>Julian . . . That's Mr. Julian.

2097
01:49:03.514 --> 01:49:06.333
He wanted [show his?] daddy last week.

2098
01:49:06.333 --> 01:49:07.139
Last Saturday.

2099
01:49:07.139 --> 01:49:11.167
"Mama say cut that off of his . . ." He cut it.

2100
01:49:11.167 --> 01:49:14.389
Charles cut it and Julian was so mad.

2101
01:49:14.389 --> 01:49:21.236
<v CH. GORDWIN>That was the first time my son . . . I’ve ever saw my son upset and he was distraught.

2102
01:49:21.236 --> 01:49:22.042
He's thirteen.

2103
01:49:22.042 --> 01:49:26.472
He's getting to that age to where the hair is everything.

2104
01:49:26.472 --> 01:49:31.306
I've been on him about his hair since he was a kid.

2105
01:49:31.306 --> 01:49:32.514
<v GORDWIN>He stay well-groomed . . .

2106
01:49:32.514 --> 01:49:33.722
<v CH. GORDWIN>He stay well-groomed.

2107
01:49:33.722 --> 01:49:34.931
<v GORDWIN>. . . all the time.

2108
01:49:34.931 --> 01:49:36.542
He dress really neat.

2109
01:49:36.542 --> 01:49:39.361
<v CH. GORDWIN>That's one thing Mama instilled in us.

2110
01:49:39.361 --> 01:49:45.000
That’s for . . . My grandmother, she instilled in us, all of us to always . . .

2111
01:49:45.000 --> 01:49:46.936
<v GORDWIN>You got to be clean and neat.

2112
01:49:46.936 --> 01:49:53.298
I don't care if you got the same dress or the same shoes you keep them clean, and you keep your clothes clean.

2113
01:49:53.298 --> 01:49:55.234
<v CH. GORDWIN>Okay well we’re going to [?] this thing.

2114
01:49:55.234 --> 01:50:04.915
<v CRAMER>Well listen I just want to say for the record on behalf of the Williams' Center we really appreciate you sharing your stories with us and I'm real excited to go back and listen to

2115
01:50:04.915 --> 01:50:05.191
<c.p>them.

2116
01:50:05.191 --> 01:50:10.447
It's just chock full of so much information and I can't wait to come back and do part two.

2117
01:50:10.447 --> 01:50:13.213
Thank you so much for inviting us to your home.

2118
01:50:13.213 --> 01:50:14.319
<v GORDWIN>Oh it’s . . . You're welcome.

2119
01:50:14.319 --> 01:50:17.638
Very much so because this is what I've been wanting to do.

2120
01:50:17.638 --> 01:50:19.298
<v CRAMER>Well I'm certainly glad that we . . .

2121
01:50:19.298 --> 01:50:20.404
<v GORDWIN>And I'm so glad.

2122
01:50:20.404 --> 01:50:23.170
I give really my best regards and thanks to Mister . . .

2123
01:50:23.170 --> 01:50:24.000
<v MCKEE>Mike Hayes.

2124
01:50:24.000 --> 01:50:32.537
<v GORDWIN>Mike Hayes for doing this for . . . I say for me because I know I asked and he told me I was the first one that brought it up.

2125
01:50:32.537 --> 01:50:35.890
And I think brother caught onto it and start saying it.

2126
01:50:35.890 --> 01:50:36.195
<v CH. GORDWIN>Butch.

2127
01:50:36.195 --> 01:50:36.805
<v GORDWIN>My brother.

2128
01:50:36.805 --> 01:50:41.378
<v CRAMER>Well it's a good idea so you would hope it would catch like fire right.

2129
01:50:41.378 --> 01:50:47.171
So thank you again so much we will be in contact with you to set up interview number two.

2130
01:50:47.171 --> 01:50:47.476
<v GORDWIN>Okay.

2131
01:50:47.476 --> 01:50:49.000
<v CRAMER>This concludes this interview.

2132
01:50:49.000 --> 01:50:49.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4409.

2133
01:50:49.000 --> 01:50:49.200
End Session I.]
